WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.455 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.455 --> 00:00:03.928 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.928 --> 00:00:05.401 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.401 --> 00:00:12.770 align:middle line:90%


00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:13.700 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.610 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:25.970 align:middle line:84%
So we're heading in to our
last of the as-yet intractable

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:26.520 align:middle line:90%
dilemmas.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.895 align:middle line:84%
We talked about the curse of
the familiar, EdTech Matthew

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:31.620 align:middle line:84%
Effect, the trap of
routine assessment,

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:36.350 align:middle line:84%
and, today, the toxic power
of data and experiment.

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:38.790 align:middle line:90%
Let's do this as an exercise.

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:40.380 align:middle line:90%
Grab one other person.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:44.780 align:middle line:84%
Your goal is to make a
list as long as possible.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:50.850 align:middle line:84%
What is all the data
that MIT has about you?

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:54.810 align:middle line:84%
If you could go through
every server that is somehow

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:58.520 align:middle line:84%
even tangentially under
the control of MIT,

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:02.120 align:middle line:84%
what are all the different
types and sources

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:05.670 align:middle line:84%
and nature and example of
data that MIT has about you?

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:06.840 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.840 align:middle line:84%
Make the list as long as
you can, as fast as you can.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:10.340 align:middle line:90%
Ready?

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:13.040 align:middle line:90%
Go.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.540 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:20.507 align:middle line:84%
Let's start aggregating some
big categories of things.

00:01:20.507 --> 00:01:21.840 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Financial information.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.540 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: So they have a
lot of financial information.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.020 align:middle line:84%
Maybe just what
are a few examples

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:31.972 align:middle line:84%
of some of the most sensitive
or personal parts of that?

00:01:31.972 --> 00:01:33.887 align:middle line:90%
They know your parents' income.

00:01:33.887 --> 00:01:35.220 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: They know your income.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:36.190 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Your income.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:37.820 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: They know
your bank account.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:38.850 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Bank account.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:40.308 align:middle line:84%
They know your bank
account number,

00:01:40.308 --> 00:01:41.590 align:middle line:90%
or you give them literally--

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:41.880 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.797 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: But they
don't have a screenshot

00:01:43.797 --> 00:01:45.420 align:middle line:90%
of your [INAUDIBLE].

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.020 align:middle line:84%
So they know a lot of
financial things about you.

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:49.622 align:middle line:90%
What are some other categories?

00:01:49.622 --> 00:01:50.330 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Health.

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:53.120 align:middle line:90%


00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.670 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: What are some
sensitive things in there?

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:57.350 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: History.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.030 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: History.

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:00.350 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Vaccinations.

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:03.740 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Vaccinations,
prescriptions.

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:04.650 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Allergies.

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:08.638 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Allergies,
mental health visits.

00:02:08.638 --> 00:02:09.680 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Your insurance.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:11.450 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Insurance.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.400 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You can also
tell if people are LGBT

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.070 align:middle line:90%
from their medical records.

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:22.890 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Sexual
orientation, activity.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:24.656 align:middle line:90%
What else you got?

00:02:24.656 --> 00:02:28.358 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Personal information,
like your name and age.

00:02:28.358 --> 00:02:29.150 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:32.225 align:middle line:84%
Let's call it
registrar information.

00:02:32.225 --> 00:02:33.600 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Social
Security number.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:34.808 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Social Security number.

00:02:34.808 --> 00:02:37.325 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH:
Address, SSN, phone.

00:02:37.325 --> 00:02:40.970 align:middle line:90%


00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:44.840 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Oh, they might
have their travel data.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:46.987 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, if
[INAUDIBLE] your travels.

00:02:46.987 --> 00:02:48.320 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: You have to register--

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:50.660 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If you
register it yourself--

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:52.880 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Oh, really?

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.317 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: MIT knows what I did.

00:02:55.317 --> 00:02:57.400 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Especially if
you've been international.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.770 align:middle line:84%
If you do it through the
school, you have to register.

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:00.720 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: You have to.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.180 align:middle line:84%
Or if you're going to a country
that they have warnings for,

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:05.840 align:middle line:84%
you have to be
like, I was there.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:09.060 align:middle line:90%


00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:10.800 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: What else we got?

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.070 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Academic records.

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:14.790 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:15.840 align:middle line:90%
That's part of it.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.660 align:middle line:90%
Academic records.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:20.950 align:middle line:84%
What would that
include these days?

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:22.360 align:middle line:90%
They know your grades.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:23.358 align:middle line:90%
What else?

00:03:23.358 --> 00:03:25.900 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Registrations, like
what classes you signed up for.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:28.020 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Registration.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:29.100 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Test scores.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.383 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Test scores.

00:03:30.383 --> 00:03:31.050 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Major.

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:37.770 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Every click
ever in Canvas, attendance.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:39.990 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:42.660 align:middle line:84%
Academic records is
a good example of one

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:47.370 align:middle line:84%
in which the granularity of
data over the last 10 years

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:49.680 align:middle line:90%
has transformed dramatically.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:53.260 align:middle line:84%
If we had had this discussion in
1980, they would have been like,

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:54.930 align:middle line:84%
I guess they know
what my grades were

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:57.060 align:middle line:84%
and what classes
I signed up for.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:00.000 align:middle line:84%
But the volume of data about
every activity that you've ever

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.920 align:middle line:84%
taken online in
any of your classes

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.390 align:middle line:84%
is substantially higher
than it used to be.

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:08.350 align:middle line:84%
Those records might not
be maintained forever.

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:10.440 align:middle line:84%
Maybe all those sites get
deleted every 18 months

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:11.880 align:middle line:90%
or so, something like that.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:14.610 align:middle line:84%
But some of them might be
kept for longer for research

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:15.780 align:middle line:90%
or other things like that.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:17.350 align:middle line:90%
All right, other categories?

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:21.348 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Residential,
home and current.

00:04:21.348 --> 00:04:22.140 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Home.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:23.830 align:middle line:90%
Let's make it even bigger.

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:26.400 align:middle line:90%
Let's say location.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:35.070 align:middle line:84%
So they know your
residence, home and campus,

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:43.470 align:middle line:84%
track your devices on Wi-Fi,
card swipes in buildings.

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:48.072 align:middle line:84%
So essentially, as you
move around campus,

00:04:48.072 --> 00:04:49.530 align:middle line:84%
somewhere, there
are databases that

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:51.660 align:middle line:90%
record all of those activities.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:53.290 align:middle line:84%
How long they maintain
those materials,

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:55.250 align:middle line:90%
we don't necessarily know.

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:57.000 align:middle line:84%
I'm sure they'd tell
us if we asked, but--

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.522 align:middle line:90%
what else they got?

00:04:59.522 --> 00:05:01.230 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: On that
location, they can also

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:04.308 align:middle line:90%
tell who you hang out with.

00:05:04.308 --> 00:05:05.100 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:06.540 align:middle line:90%
I just learned about this today.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:08.323 align:middle line:84%
What's the system
where you add people?

00:05:08.323 --> 00:05:09.240 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Guest lists.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:09.790 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: OK.

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:11.373 align:middle line:84%
So I guess if you
live in a residence,

00:05:11.373 --> 00:05:13.350 align:middle line:84%
you create a guest
list of people

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:15.450 align:middle line:90%
that are allowed to visit you.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:18.120 align:middle line:84%
You could just reconstitute the
entire social network of MIT

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:19.130 align:middle line:90%
from the guest list.

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:22.230 align:middle line:90%


00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:24.765 align:middle line:84%
There's all kinds of things
you could learn from that.

00:05:24.765 --> 00:05:26.640 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: There's
extracurricular activities.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:33.670 align:middle line:84%
So they know what sports teams
and athletics stats on that.

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:38.063 align:middle line:84%
And then if you signed up for
club teams, or just clubs.

00:05:38.063 --> 00:05:40.230 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: It would be
all those kinds of things.

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:42.060 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: PE, as well.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:49.140 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, your gym
membership swipes, activity.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:50.870 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Political
affiliations.

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:53.370 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: How would they
get your political affiliation?

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:54.580 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Through
your extracurriculars,

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:56.700 align:middle line:84%
like if you're involved
with the recent stuff

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:59.588 align:middle line:90%
that's been going on on campus.

00:05:59.588 --> 00:06:00.630 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, good.

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:05.520 align:middle line:84%
So there are
increasingly, presumably,

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:09.800 align:middle line:84%
lists of people who are
affiliated with pro-Palestinian,

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:11.800 align:middle line:84%
pro-Israeli, any of those
other kinds of things,

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:13.660 align:middle line:84%
but also people that have
just been able to tell before,

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:14.368 align:middle line:90%
if you remember--

00:06:14.368 --> 00:06:17.010 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, I assume there's
a Democrat and Republican club

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:21.090 align:middle line:90%
at MIT, I would imagine--

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:22.320 align:middle line:90%
someone, somewhere.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:23.200 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Emails.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:24.040 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yes.

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:26.820 align:middle line:84%
They have the entirety
of your email record.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:29.820 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Even broader,
just online activity.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:32.100 align:middle line:84%
Because you have emails,
websites, everything

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:34.200 align:middle line:90%
you've registered for.

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:37.080 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: They also offer a VPN.

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:42.150 align:middle line:84%
So it's secure to
everyone but MIT.

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:44.340 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: They also
have your Google Drive.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:46.980 align:middle line:84%
If you sign up for
the MIT Google Drive,

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:49.135 align:middle line:90%
they have all your documents.

00:06:49.135 --> 00:06:50.260 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Google Drive.

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:53.520 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I had a
congressional committee

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:55.950 align:middle line:84%
that wanted to find out
more about my work, not

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:59.940 align:middle line:84%
necessarily in a generous
way, this past year.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:03.630 align:middle line:84%
And the Office of
General Counsel

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:06.270 align:middle line:84%
did a whole search
throughout my email

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:09.030 align:middle line:84%
to find terms that this
congressional committee was

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:11.040 align:middle line:84%
interested in
learning more about.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:14.470 align:middle line:84%
And we were going
back and forth.

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:17.260 align:middle line:84%
And I was like, what
do you need from me?

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:20.050 align:middle line:84%
And they were like, well, it's
better if you help us with this.

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:23.250 align:middle line:84%
But either way,
we're going to do it.

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:28.120 align:middle line:84%
So all the records
that you generate--

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:33.000 align:middle line:84%
and that's a good
example of how even--

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:36.150 align:middle line:84%
I'm reasonably confident
that in most circumstances--

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:38.380 align:middle line:84%
MIT is not going through my
email every day and like,

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:39.430 align:middle line:90%
what's Justin up to?

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:41.130 align:middle line:90%
They don't care.

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:44.760 align:middle line:84%
But simply having those records
allows all kinds of things

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:46.010 align:middle line:90%
to happen with those records--

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:50.190 align:middle line:90%


00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:52.145 align:middle line:84%
do you guys have automated
laundry machines?

00:07:52.145 --> 00:07:52.770 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:55.890 align:middle line:90%


00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:59.130 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Washing frequency.

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:00.598 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's an app.

00:08:00.598 --> 00:08:02.140 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah,
it's even an app.

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:02.970 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, it's an app.

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:04.530 align:middle line:84%
You pay for your
machine, and then you--

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:06.905 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And you have to sign
up through your MIT email.

00:08:06.905 --> 00:08:08.982 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, you have
to do it through MIT.

00:08:08.982 --> 00:08:10.440 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: And
so that, I think,

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:13.020 align:middle line:90%
also raises some questions of--

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:16.290 align:middle line:84%
it's possible, actually, that
MIT doesn't have your laundry

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:18.450 align:middle line:84%
data but that MIT
just somehow mandates

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:23.790 align:middle line:84%
that a third party has all of
your laundry data, which, again.

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:26.290 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How about keys to
your room if you live on campus?

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:27.370 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:28.620 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Oh, like physically.

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:32.940 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah-- keys.

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:35.307 align:middle line:84%
What's good about
them having keys?

00:08:35.307 --> 00:08:36.640 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: If you get locked out.

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:37.450 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:38.242 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:08:38.242 --> 00:08:43.165 align:middle line:84%
So if there's a day that
you're locked out of your room,

00:08:43.165 --> 00:08:44.790 align:middle line:84%
you're going to be
like, it is so great

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:47.340 align:middle line:84%
that MIT knows exactly
what key goes in my door

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:49.870 align:middle line:84%
and can make another
one of those.

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:52.510 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: They had room
inspections a few weeks ago

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:54.580 align:middle line:84%
where they went into
every person's room

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:57.658 align:middle line:84%
to check if they were
abiding by the rules.

00:08:57.658 --> 00:08:58.700 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, good.

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:04.832 align:middle line:84%
What might be good about room
inspections for everyone?

00:09:04.832 --> 00:09:05.540 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Safety.

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:06.665 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, safety.

00:09:06.665 --> 00:09:11.537 align:middle line:84%
If your neighbors are
having halogen lamps that

00:09:11.537 --> 00:09:13.120 align:middle line:84%
can catch on fire
and stuff like that,

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:16.178 align:middle line:84%
you would be glad that
they knew about that.

00:09:16.178 --> 00:09:18.220 align:middle line:84%
But obviously, it also
seems like a huge invasion

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:20.120 align:middle line:90%
of your privacy.

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:27.070 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I would say what also
made the room inspections better

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:31.400 align:middle line:84%
is also you're trusting MIT
to be responsible with it.

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:34.750 align:middle line:84%
So they told everyone
ahead of time,

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:38.590 align:middle line:84%
and they made sure that
it's a visual inspection.

00:09:38.590 --> 00:09:41.120 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, they're not
going through your stuff.

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:43.872 align:middle line:84%
But obviously that's not a
thing for a lot of colleges.

00:09:43.872 --> 00:09:46.330 align:middle line:84%
A lot of colleges can just like
come barging into your room

00:09:46.330 --> 00:09:47.368 align:middle line:90%
and look at anything.

00:09:47.368 --> 00:09:48.160 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:51.130 align:middle line:84%
So institutions make
choices about how--

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:52.450 align:middle line:90%
so, hopefully, I don't know.

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:54.340 align:middle line:84%
What kind of
feelings do you have

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:58.464 align:middle line:84%
when you start looking
at this completed list?

00:09:58.464 --> 00:10:01.840 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: There's
some more I have.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:04.070 align:middle line:84%
The one that I've been
thinking about lately,

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:07.280 align:middle line:84%
given the affirmative
action stuff,

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:10.130 align:middle line:84%
is just general
demographics [INAUDIBLE].

00:10:10.130 --> 00:10:13.420 align:middle line:84%
And also, something that was
not mentioned in financial

00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:19.007 align:middle line:84%
is purchase history, so if
you buy anything on campus.

00:10:19.007 --> 00:10:20.840 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: They know
you got sushi today.

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:21.610 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:28.090 align:middle line:84%
Like, Tito and all the
coffee stores, like Dunkin',

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:31.023 align:middle line:84%
the ones in Forbes,
stuff like that.

00:10:31.023 --> 00:10:33.440 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, particularly
if those things somehow--

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:35.260 align:middle line:84%
can you put money
on your ID card

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:36.860 align:middle line:84%
and spend directly
from your ID card?

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:37.485 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:10:37.485 --> 00:10:40.030 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: So
there's two units

00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:42.190 align:middle line:84%
that we should be
thinking about anytime

00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:44.230 align:middle line:84%
we think about school
data is that schools

00:10:44.230 --> 00:10:48.100 align:middle line:84%
have a bunch of data, and then
schools authorize third parties

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:49.490 align:middle line:90%
to get a bunch of data.

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:53.510 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes only the
third party has access.

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:55.220 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes both
parties have access.

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:57.820 align:middle line:84%
Like, both Canvas and
MIT people have access

00:10:57.820 --> 00:10:59.330 align:middle line:90%
to all of your Canvas data.

00:10:59.330 --> 00:11:02.428 align:middle line:84%
The laundry app might
be a good example of--

00:11:02.428 --> 00:11:04.720 align:middle line:84%
if somebody told me that the
maintenance department had

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:07.345 align:middle line:84%
access to all the laundry data,
or IT, I wouldn't be surprised.

00:11:07.345 --> 00:11:08.803 align:middle line:84%
But if they were
like, yeah, no, we

00:11:08.803 --> 00:11:11.410 align:middle line:84%
make you sign up with an email,
but only the laundry people

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:14.440 align:middle line:84%
know your laundry data, that
wouldn't surprise me, either.

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:17.500 align:middle line:84%
But those are two useful,
salient categories

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:20.590 align:middle line:84%
that will come up as we
keep talking about things.

00:11:20.590 --> 00:11:22.270 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Your deliveries, too.

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:25.643 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Oh, yeah,
your mail deliveries.

00:11:25.643 --> 00:11:28.060 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Oh, because
things go through the mail room?

00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:28.685 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:11:28.685 --> 00:11:33.610 align:middle line:84%
So they store all of your
information and your deliveries

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:37.390 align:middle line:84%
into a tracker system and then
send you emails of your delivery

00:11:37.390 --> 00:11:37.950 align:middle line:90%
has come in.

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:38.810 align:middle line:90%
You need to pick it up.

00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:39.040 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:39.540 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:42.190 align:middle line:84%
And so, typically,
the data is exchanged

00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:43.420 align:middle line:90%
for some kind of service.

00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:45.400 align:middle line:84%
You want to know when
your new headphones are

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:46.650 align:middle line:90%
coming or something like that.

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:48.275 align:middle line:84%
And so you're glad
that they have that.

00:11:48.275 --> 00:11:50.150 align:middle line:84%
But in order for them
to be able to know

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:51.650 align:middle line:84%
when your new
headphones are coming,

00:11:51.650 --> 00:11:53.370 align:middle line:84%
and to do other kinds
of things with it--

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:56.940 align:middle line:84%
the other thing, you could
say, over the past 10 years,

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:59.390 align:middle line:84%
was it a good idea
for MIT to track

00:11:59.390 --> 00:12:01.550 align:middle line:84%
the changing volume
of deliveries

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:02.730 align:middle line:90%
to individual students?

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:03.860 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:12:03.860 --> 00:12:06.800 align:middle line:84%
I'm sure MIT has had to build
all kinds of infrastructure

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:08.690 align:middle line:84%
to make it possible
for you all to get

00:12:08.690 --> 00:12:11.000 align:middle line:90%
your stuff in a sensible way.

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:12.740 align:middle line:84%
And it's probably a
good idea that MIT

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:15.630 align:middle line:84%
has data about aggregate--
particularly in the aggregates.

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:19.490 align:middle line:84%
It's probably a good idea
to know, oh, in 2014,

00:12:19.490 --> 00:12:22.620 align:middle line:84%
the typical student got
2.3 packages a year,

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:24.960 align:middle line:84%
and now they get 30.6
packages per year.

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:27.500 align:middle line:84%
And it just requires a whole
different infrastructure

00:12:27.500 --> 00:12:31.333 align:middle line:84%
to support those two kinds of
ways of people doing commerce.

00:12:31.333 --> 00:12:32.750 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, what other
feelings do you

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:35.090 align:middle line:84%
have as you look at
the whole list of data

00:12:35.090 --> 00:12:38.748 align:middle line:90%
that MIT might have about you?

00:12:38.748 --> 00:12:40.790 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I feel like,
ultimately, I'm not really

00:12:40.790 --> 00:12:41.850 align:middle line:90%
that surprised.

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:43.840 align:middle line:84%
I feel like we grew
up in a generation

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:47.480 align:middle line:84%
where digital data has been
collected for our entire lives.

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:50.450 align:middle line:84%
It was not something that was
introduced but always there.

00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:52.540 align:middle line:84%
And so I feel like
I've kind of grown up

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:55.690 align:middle line:84%
in this world knowing that all
of this type of information

00:12:55.690 --> 00:12:58.610 align:middle line:84%
can and is being
collected on me.

00:12:58.610 --> 00:13:02.360 align:middle line:84%
And so I'm still going
to live the same way.

00:13:02.360 --> 00:13:07.840 align:middle line:84%
But I just-- it just is what
it is in kind of a sad way.

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:09.975 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH:
Yeah, there's sad--

00:13:09.975 --> 00:13:11.600 align:middle line:84%
you're clearly not
comfortable with it.

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:12.880 align:middle line:90%
Because if you listen to--

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:14.980 align:middle line:84%
if you stop talking for a
second while people were making

00:13:14.980 --> 00:13:17.470 align:middle line:84%
the list and listen, there's
all kinds of awkward laughter

00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:18.890 align:middle line:84%
that happens throughout
the whole time.

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:20.848 align:middle line:84%
There's something about
this which is like, oh,

00:13:20.848 --> 00:13:22.000 align:middle line:90%
this doesn't feel good.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:24.950 align:middle line:84%
Any individual category can
make you not feel that great.

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:27.460 align:middle line:84%
Do you really want
MIT's IT department

00:13:27.460 --> 00:13:29.410 align:middle line:84%
to be able to track
where everyone can go?

00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:31.720 align:middle line:84%
That seems like
it could go awry.

00:13:31.720 --> 00:13:35.210 align:middle line:84%
And then just the whole
volume in aggregate,

00:13:35.210 --> 00:13:38.050 align:middle line:90%
you realize how much--

00:13:38.050 --> 00:13:41.625 align:middle line:84%
you could make a very similar
list for K-12 schools, as well.

00:13:41.625 --> 00:13:44.000 align:middle line:84%
You can probably guess the
places in which things are not

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:45.030 align:middle line:90%
exactly the same.

00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:48.000 align:middle line:90%


00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:49.970 align:middle line:84%
And the data collection
that people can do

00:13:49.970 --> 00:13:52.010 align:middle line:84%
can be really
influenced by policy.

00:13:52.010 --> 00:13:54.620 align:middle line:84%
States have different
rules about how attendance

00:13:54.620 --> 00:13:57.140 align:middle line:90%
is connected to funding.

00:13:57.140 --> 00:13:58.800 align:middle line:90%
Who's there on a particular day?

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:01.250 align:middle line:84%
Who's there for what
periods of time?

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:03.938 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine that you want
to make sure that if a kid moves

00:14:03.938 --> 00:14:06.230 align:middle line:84%
schools three times in a
school year that not all three

00:14:06.230 --> 00:14:09.590 align:middle line:84%
of those schools get like a full
student's tuition worth of--

00:14:09.590 --> 00:14:12.650 align:middle line:90%
or a per-pupil expenditure.

00:14:12.650 --> 00:14:14.900 align:middle line:84%
But there are states that
part of the reason why

00:14:14.900 --> 00:14:17.582 align:middle line:84%
they issue ID cards to students
and track their entrance

00:14:17.582 --> 00:14:19.790 align:middle line:84%
and things like that is to
pay really close attention

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:23.090 align:middle line:84%
to that attendance data, just
for that particular policy

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:23.970 align:middle line:90%
purpose.

00:14:23.970 --> 00:14:25.890 align:middle line:90%
Was there another?

00:14:25.890 --> 00:14:28.170 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Adding on to
it being not surprising,

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:30.500 align:middle line:84%
I would say a lot
of the information

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:35.010 align:middle line:84%
that they took
while we were here,

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:37.880 align:middle line:84%
like current residence
or laundry and stuff

00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:42.680 align:middle line:84%
like that, I feel like is
much more unsurprising just

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:47.480 align:middle line:84%
because it is something
that is novel when you come

00:14:47.480 --> 00:14:51.090 align:middle line:84%
to a college or an institution
like this, where it's like, oh,

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:51.590 align:middle line:90%
wow.

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:53.900 align:middle line:84%
They really do have
this information.

00:14:53.900 --> 00:14:56.990 align:middle line:84%
But I think what's
kind of a little

00:14:56.990 --> 00:15:00.080 align:middle line:84%
crazy is how much of this
information they have

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:02.870 align:middle line:90%
before you even get here.

00:15:02.870 --> 00:15:04.640 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: So
yeah, for applicants,

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:08.810 align:middle line:84%
for people who are admitted into
the school and things like that.

00:15:08.810 --> 00:15:11.060 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, there's a lot that you
need to tell MIT for them

00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:13.170 align:middle line:84%
to be able to come
here and get here.

00:15:13.170 --> 00:15:15.335 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot that you
need to be able to--

00:15:15.335 --> 00:15:19.130 align:middle line:90%


00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:20.072 align:middle line:90%
to get in.

00:15:20.072 --> 00:15:21.530 align:middle line:84%
Again, these days,
you have to take

00:15:21.530 --> 00:15:23.930 align:middle line:84%
the SAT, which means
you are required

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:26.660 align:middle line:84%
to give a bunch of information
to the College Board.

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:29.142 align:middle line:84%
And you also, in the
process of doing that,

00:15:29.142 --> 00:15:31.350 align:middle line:84%
you're probably asked to
opt in to a bunch of things.

00:15:31.350 --> 00:15:32.467 align:middle line:90%
I had a master's student--

00:15:32.467 --> 00:15:34.050 align:middle line:84%
I can't remember all
the details now--

00:15:34.050 --> 00:15:37.880 align:middle line:84%
but did their thesis on,
essentially, the kinds of things

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:40.730 align:middle line:84%
that the College Board did
with information that they

00:15:40.730 --> 00:15:44.330 align:middle line:84%
got, which, to some extent,
you could say something like,

00:15:44.330 --> 00:15:46.827 align:middle line:84%
if you opt in to
flowers.com or something

00:15:46.827 --> 00:15:48.410 align:middle line:84%
like that-- you're
like, man, I really

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:51.090 align:middle line:84%
want good information about
what flowers are available,

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:53.730 align:middle line:84%
and then flowers.com
sells your data.

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:58.010 align:middle line:84%
You're like, well, maybe that's
a terrible system to exist in.

00:15:58.010 --> 00:16:01.630 align:middle line:84%
But flowers.com did ask me if
I wanted to give them my data,

00:16:01.630 --> 00:16:03.630 align:middle line:84%
and I did agree to let
them do anything with it.

00:16:03.630 --> 00:16:05.755 align:middle line:84%
And that was just my
voluntary relationship.

00:16:05.755 --> 00:16:07.880 align:middle line:84%
But you didn't really have
a voluntary relationship

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:09.650 align:middle line:90%
with the College Board.

00:16:09.650 --> 00:16:12.195 align:middle line:84%
You had no choice but to
interact with the College Board

00:16:12.195 --> 00:16:14.070 align:middle line:84%
to make it through this
major life milestone.

00:16:14.070 --> 00:16:16.640 align:middle line:84%
And so, to the extent that
the College Board does things

00:16:16.640 --> 00:16:19.718 align:middle line:84%
with your information, that's
a different relationship

00:16:19.718 --> 00:16:21.260 align:middle line:84%
that you have with
them than you have

00:16:21.260 --> 00:16:25.400 align:middle line:84%
with flowers.com or pets.com
or your own favorite thing.

00:16:25.400 --> 00:16:28.220 align:middle line:84%
So we live in a world
in which schools

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:32.270 align:middle line:84%
have enormous amounts
of information

00:16:32.270 --> 00:16:35.600 align:middle line:84%
about their individual
students, that there's probably

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:38.880 align:middle line:84%
a pretty big generational
shifts in that experience.

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:45.000 align:middle line:84%
One of the main laws that
governs these records is FERPA,

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:46.890 align:middle line:84%
the Federal Education
Records Protection Act,

00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:51.410 align:middle line:84%
which I think was
authored in the 1970s.

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:53.000 align:middle line:84%
We basically have
a legal framework

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:56.030 align:middle line:84%
for supervising this
data, which was just built

00:16:56.030 --> 00:16:59.230 align:middle line:90%
in an entirely different era.

00:16:59.230 --> 00:17:02.210 align:middle line:84%
FERPA refers to this thing
called student records.

00:17:02.210 --> 00:17:05.359 align:middle line:84%
At the time, it would
have been very, very clear

00:17:05.359 --> 00:17:06.540 align:middle line:90%
what a student record was.

00:17:06.540 --> 00:17:08.690 align:middle line:84%
You would have gone to
the registrar's office,

00:17:08.690 --> 00:17:10.710 align:middle line:84%
and there would have
been big file drawers.

00:17:10.710 --> 00:17:12.210 align:middle line:84%
And you would have
pulled out a file

00:17:12.210 --> 00:17:14.510 align:middle line:84%
and been like, that
is the student record.

00:17:14.510 --> 00:17:17.430 align:middle line:84%
Are your clicker questions
part of your student record?

00:17:17.430 --> 00:17:20.160 align:middle line:90%
Are your Codio assignments?

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:21.960 align:middle line:90%
Are your Canvas entries?

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:24.234 align:middle line:90%
Are your Piazza forum things?

00:17:24.234 --> 00:17:25.859 align:middle line:84%
Are those part of
your student records?

00:17:25.859 --> 00:17:29.660 align:middle line:84%
There's much less
clarity over those things

00:17:29.660 --> 00:17:31.075 align:middle line:90%
than one might think.

00:17:31.075 --> 00:17:38.760 align:middle line:90%


00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:41.397 align:middle line:84%
The term "toxic" has a kind
of negative connotation,

00:17:41.397 --> 00:17:43.730 align:middle line:84%
but I borrowed it from a
security researcher named Bruce

00:17:43.730 --> 00:17:44.690 align:middle line:90%
Schneier.

00:17:44.690 --> 00:17:50.240 align:middle line:84%
And part of his argument was
that toxic things are powerful.

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:53.730 align:middle line:84%
Chemotherapies are toxic,
and they save your life.

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:54.500 align:middle line:90%
They're great.

00:17:54.500 --> 00:17:56.270 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes it's really
good to interact

00:17:56.270 --> 00:17:58.190 align:middle line:90%
with powerful, toxic things.

00:17:58.190 --> 00:18:00.380 align:middle line:84%
But toxic things
are also dangerous.

00:18:00.380 --> 00:18:03.050 align:middle line:84%
In particular, I
think most of us

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:06.360 align:middle line:84%
have an inclination that
when something is toxic,

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:07.887 align:middle line:84%
you should not leave
it lying around

00:18:07.887 --> 00:18:08.970 align:middle line:90%
for a long period of time.

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:10.740 align:middle line:84%
That was the heart
of Bruce's argument

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:18.410 align:middle line:84%
was that companies were making
a big mistake by having a very

00:18:18.410 --> 00:18:21.303 align:middle line:84%
capacious approach to the
collection and storage of data,

00:18:21.303 --> 00:18:22.970 align:middle line:84%
that, actually, what
you should be doing

00:18:22.970 --> 00:18:24.970 align:middle line:84%
is just taking the data
you need and getting rid

00:18:24.970 --> 00:18:26.870 align:middle line:84%
of the rest of it because
it's a toxic asset

00:18:26.870 --> 00:18:27.780 align:middle line:90%
to have lying around.

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:28.920 align:middle line:84%
The more you have
it lying around,

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:30.770 align:middle line:84%
the more you're
vulnerable to breaches,

00:18:30.770 --> 00:18:34.250 align:middle line:84%
to other kinds of actors
doing various nefarious

00:18:34.250 --> 00:18:37.640 align:middle line:90%
things with that data.

00:18:37.640 --> 00:18:40.610 align:middle line:84%
If you're an
educational researcher,

00:18:40.610 --> 00:18:43.070 align:middle line:84%
what kinds of thoughts
might you have

00:18:43.070 --> 00:18:44.988 align:middle line:84%
when you see this
giant list of the data

00:18:44.988 --> 00:18:46.405 align:middle line:84%
that schools collect
about people?

00:18:46.405 --> 00:18:53.097 align:middle line:90%


00:18:53.097 --> 00:18:55.430 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Honestly, the first
thing that came to my mind

00:18:55.430 --> 00:18:58.550 align:middle line:84%
was excitement in the
sense of if you're

00:18:58.550 --> 00:19:01.100 align:middle line:84%
forming any type of
research and trying

00:19:01.100 --> 00:19:05.180 align:middle line:84%
to study how anything
about a person

00:19:05.180 --> 00:19:07.920 align:middle line:84%
can affect whatever
you're studying.

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:11.630 align:middle line:84%
If you have all of
this data, then there's

00:19:11.630 --> 00:19:17.360 align:middle line:84%
really no limit to what
you're able to research.

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:20.330 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: This was
an article from today--

00:19:20.330 --> 00:19:21.030 align:middle line:90%
two days ago.

00:19:21.030 --> 00:19:23.280 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to give you a
couple of cutting-edge things.

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:25.260 align:middle line:84%
"Middle schools in Norway
banned smartphones.

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:28.130 align:middle line:84%
The benefits were
dramatic, a study shows."

00:19:28.130 --> 00:19:31.520 align:middle line:84%
A study published
earlier this year

00:19:31.520 --> 00:19:35.460 align:middle line:84%
from a Norwegian
doctoral student,

00:19:35.460 --> 00:19:38.610 align:middle line:84%
where there were hundreds
of schools across Norway

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:42.930 align:middle line:84%
that, in various ways,
banned smartphones.

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:44.820 align:middle line:84%
Some of the outcomes
they look at

00:19:44.820 --> 00:19:50.400 align:middle line:84%
are student outcomes like
learning but also mental health.

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:52.710 align:middle line:84%
"I show that banning
smartphones significantly

00:19:52.710 --> 00:19:54.300 align:middle line:84%
decreases the
health care take-up

00:19:54.300 --> 00:19:57.670 align:middle line:84%
for psychological symptoms
and diseases among girls.

00:19:57.670 --> 00:20:00.700 align:middle line:84%
Post-ban bullying among
both genders decreases.

00:20:00.700 --> 00:20:01.685 align:middle line:90%
Girls' GPA improves.

00:20:01.685 --> 00:20:03.060 align:middle line:84%
And their likelihood
of attending

00:20:03.060 --> 00:20:05.080 align:middle line:84%
an academic high
school track increases.

00:20:05.080 --> 00:20:08.640 align:middle line:84%
These effects are larger from
girls from low socioeconomic

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:09.690 align:middle line:90%
backgrounds."

00:20:09.690 --> 00:20:12.863 align:middle line:84%
So in Norway, they have vast
informations about students.

00:20:12.863 --> 00:20:14.280 align:middle line:84%
Here are some
things that you need

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:15.613 align:middle line:90%
to know to make this study work.

00:20:15.613 --> 00:20:17.380 align:middle line:84%
You need to know all
the students' grades.

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:21.250 align:middle line:84%
You need to know where all
the students go to college.

00:20:21.250 --> 00:20:23.850 align:middle line:84%
You need to know whenever
the students visit

00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:28.410 align:middle line:84%
the Norwegian health care system
and what kinds of requests

00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:30.990 align:middle line:84%
they're making from the
Norwegian health care system.

00:20:30.990 --> 00:20:33.610 align:middle line:84%
And you need to have
them, every year,

00:20:33.610 --> 00:20:36.450 align:middle line:84%
take a well-being survey that
includes a bunch of questions

00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:37.620 align:middle line:90%
about bullying.

00:20:37.620 --> 00:20:39.480 align:middle line:84%
That's a lot of
information for the state

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:41.230 align:middle line:90%
to collect about these students.

00:20:41.230 --> 00:20:45.330 align:middle line:84%
But it certainly seems to me
like a pretty pro-social thing

00:20:45.330 --> 00:20:48.780 align:middle line:84%
to be able to say, hey,
if we look at the schools

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:51.900 align:middle line:84%
that ban smartphones in middle
school versus the ones that

00:20:51.900 --> 00:20:55.270 align:middle line:84%
don't, there seem to be all
of these health outcomes.

00:20:55.270 --> 00:20:58.470 align:middle line:84%
And we can even, actually, say
that the health-- it's not just

00:20:58.470 --> 00:21:00.460 align:middle line:84%
academic outcomes, it's
also health outcomes,

00:21:00.460 --> 00:21:02.520 align:middle line:84%
although the health outcomes are
probably related, in some way,

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:03.790 align:middle line:90%
to the academic outcomes.

00:21:03.790 --> 00:21:05.220 align:middle line:90%
But it's particularly for girls.

00:21:05.220 --> 00:21:07.830 align:middle line:84%
And it's particularly
for students

00:21:07.830 --> 00:21:09.900 align:middle line:84%
in low socioeconomic
environments.

00:21:09.900 --> 00:21:12.550 align:middle line:84%
That seems pretty
useful stuff to know.

00:21:12.550 --> 00:21:17.010 align:middle line:84%
And so that is the kind of
trade-off that people would face

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:20.730 align:middle line:84%
as they're going back
and forth between--

00:21:20.730 --> 00:21:23.940 align:middle line:84%
the mass of data that we
collect about students

00:21:23.940 --> 00:21:26.882 align:middle line:84%
is also what lets us do
pretty sophisticated,

00:21:26.882 --> 00:21:28.590 align:middle line:84%
complicated research
about those students

00:21:28.590 --> 00:21:30.520 align:middle line:84%
to be able to make claims
about how well they're

00:21:30.520 --> 00:21:32.103 align:middle line:84%
doing in various
parts of their lives.

00:21:32.103 --> 00:21:37.060 align:middle line:90%


00:21:37.060 --> 00:21:38.480 align:middle line:90%
Toxic power of data experiment.

00:21:38.480 --> 00:21:40.600 align:middle line:84%
All right, I'll tell you
about at least a couple

00:21:40.600 --> 00:21:43.090 align:middle line:90%
of other things.

00:21:43.090 --> 00:21:49.210 align:middle line:84%
There was a study that you read
about in the chapter last night.

00:21:49.210 --> 00:21:52.120 align:middle line:84%
This is a headline that
came out-- it was in 2018--

00:21:52.120 --> 00:21:53.870 align:middle line:84%
this is a headline
that came out about it.

00:21:53.870 --> 00:21:56.650 align:middle line:84%
"Pearson Tested
Social-Psychological Messages

00:21:56.650 --> 00:21:59.830 align:middle line:84%
in Learning Software,
with Mixed Results."

00:21:59.830 --> 00:22:02.120 align:middle line:84%
Did we talk about
mindset in this class?

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:05.147 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we did briefly-- growth
mindset and fixed mindset?

00:22:05.147 --> 00:22:05.980 align:middle line:90%
Something like that.

00:22:05.980 --> 00:22:07.450 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, you're probably
all familiar with it

00:22:07.450 --> 00:22:09.970 align:middle line:84%
because teachers talk about
it all the time, but there--

00:22:09.970 --> 00:22:12.730 align:middle line:84%
or education
researchers, other folks.

00:22:12.730 --> 00:22:14.590 align:middle line:84%
There are some
social psychologists

00:22:14.590 --> 00:22:18.165 align:middle line:84%
who have identified
two kinds of mindsets

00:22:18.165 --> 00:22:19.790 align:middle line:84%
that people often
have towards learning

00:22:19.790 --> 00:22:20.930 align:middle line:90%
or other kinds of things.

00:22:20.930 --> 00:22:23.152 align:middle line:84%
One is a fixed mindset,
which generally says,

00:22:23.152 --> 00:22:25.360 align:middle line:84%
some people are smart, and
some people are not smart.

00:22:25.360 --> 00:22:28.757 align:middle line:84%
And a growth mindset
suggests that people

00:22:28.757 --> 00:22:30.840 align:middle line:84%
have fluid intelligence,
and you can make yourself

00:22:30.840 --> 00:22:32.590 align:middle line:84%
more intelligent and
other kinds of things

00:22:32.590 --> 00:22:37.290 align:middle line:84%
Seems to be a pretty good idea
to help young people adopt

00:22:37.290 --> 00:22:40.560 align:middle line:84%
the latter mindset rather
than the former mindset.

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:43.260 align:middle line:84%
Better to have them have a
growth mindset rather than

00:22:43.260 --> 00:22:44.080 align:middle line:90%
a fixed mindset.

00:22:44.080 --> 00:22:49.660 align:middle line:84%
Exactly how much it matters is
a subject of enormous debate.

00:22:49.660 --> 00:22:51.780 align:middle line:84%
There were a number
of early studies

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:54.630 align:middle line:84%
that suggested it could matter
quite enormously to all kinds

00:22:54.630 --> 00:22:55.330 align:middle line:90%
of people.

00:22:55.330 --> 00:22:57.450 align:middle line:84%
And some of the more
recent, larger studies

00:22:57.450 --> 00:23:00.030 align:middle line:84%
suggest that the
best things that we

00:23:00.030 --> 00:23:02.760 align:middle line:84%
know how to do to improve
growth mindset only

00:23:02.760 --> 00:23:04.620 align:middle line:84%
helps some students
and maybe only

00:23:04.620 --> 00:23:08.430 align:middle line:84%
help them a little bit, like
a lot of things that we study.

00:23:08.430 --> 00:23:12.970 align:middle line:84%
So Pearson offers all
kinds of software products.

00:23:12.970 --> 00:23:18.590 align:middle line:84%
And I think this was a line
of computer science software

00:23:18.590 --> 00:23:19.090 align:middle line:90%
products.

00:23:19.090 --> 00:23:21.357 align:middle line:84%
And so you do a bunch
of programming exercises

00:23:21.357 --> 00:23:23.440 align:middle line:84%
and multiple-choice questions
or things like that.

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:25.650 align:middle line:84%
And the thing that
they decided to add

00:23:25.650 --> 00:23:28.750 align:middle line:84%
was not just right
or wrong messages

00:23:28.750 --> 00:23:33.180 align:middle line:84%
but some people getting growth
mindset messages and some people

00:23:33.180 --> 00:23:34.720 align:middle line:84%
not getting growth
mindset messages.

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:36.762 align:middle line:84%
So I won't get this exactly
right, but an example

00:23:36.762 --> 00:23:38.820 align:middle line:84%
of a growth mindset
message might be something

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:43.620 align:middle line:84%
like, that's not
right, but keep trying

00:23:43.620 --> 00:23:46.830 align:middle line:84%
and you can learn more,
or other things to suggest

00:23:46.830 --> 00:23:50.190 align:middle line:84%
that if you keep working
at this, you'll get better.

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:55.230 align:middle line:84%
What seems good
about implementing

00:23:55.230 --> 00:23:58.510 align:middle line:84%
that kind of intervention
in a system like this,

00:23:58.510 --> 00:24:01.650 align:middle line:84%
and what seems problematic about
implementing an intervention

00:24:01.650 --> 00:24:02.705 align:middle line:90%
in a system like this?

00:24:02.705 --> 00:24:06.720 align:middle line:90%


00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:09.540 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well, problematic
is that the control group is,

00:24:09.540 --> 00:24:13.890 align:middle line:84%
essentially, not necessarily
being hurt but not benefiting

00:24:13.890 --> 00:24:14.770 align:middle line:90%
at all.

00:24:14.770 --> 00:24:19.650 align:middle line:84%
And if you're saying that
a growth mindset really

00:24:19.650 --> 00:24:23.160 align:middle line:84%
does help people, choosing
to, essentially, not

00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:25.935 align:middle line:84%
help them by putting them in the
control group can [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:25.935 --> 00:24:27.560 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah,
that's interesting.

00:24:27.560 --> 00:24:29.650 align:middle line:84%
So Pearson goes
and reads a bunch

00:24:29.650 --> 00:24:31.240 align:middle line:84%
of research about
growth mindset.

00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:34.570 align:middle line:84%
And they're like, hey,
this growth mindset stuff

00:24:34.570 --> 00:24:36.460 align:middle line:90%
seems to work sometimes.

00:24:36.460 --> 00:24:41.090 align:middle line:84%
But they don't decide to
just add it to their product.

00:24:41.090 --> 00:24:45.190 align:middle line:84%
They add it to their product in
this experimental paradigm where

00:24:45.190 --> 00:24:48.130 align:middle line:84%
some people get it,
and some people don't.

00:24:48.130 --> 00:24:50.680 align:middle line:84%
And so people in the
same class are going

00:24:50.680 --> 00:24:52.960 align:middle line:90%
to have a different experience.

00:24:52.960 --> 00:24:56.500 align:middle line:84%
So Dana proposes that one
problem with that is that

00:24:56.500 --> 00:24:59.680 align:middle line:84%
the people who get the
experimental intervention might

00:24:59.680 --> 00:25:02.360 align:middle line:84%
benefit in the way that people
in the control group don't.

00:25:02.360 --> 00:25:04.715 align:middle line:84%
What are some other
problems that potentially

00:25:04.715 --> 00:25:05.715 align:middle line:90%
could show up with this?

00:25:05.715 --> 00:25:15.700 align:middle line:90%


00:25:15.700 --> 00:25:17.950 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I guess you have
to take into account who's

00:25:17.950 --> 00:25:20.330 align:middle line:84%
in the control group and who's
in the experimental group.

00:25:20.330 --> 00:25:23.140 align:middle line:84%
How good of a job do they do
at filtering out their students

00:25:23.140 --> 00:25:25.420 align:middle line:84%
to make sure it's
roughly equal in terms

00:25:25.420 --> 00:25:28.150 align:middle line:84%
of socioeconomic background
or prior experience

00:25:28.150 --> 00:25:29.510 align:middle line:90%
with the subject, et cetera?

00:25:29.510 --> 00:25:30.910 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: That's good.

00:25:30.910 --> 00:25:33.680 align:middle line:84%
And I would say, under
most circumstances,

00:25:33.680 --> 00:25:36.020 align:middle line:84%
especially in these
kinds of digital systems,

00:25:36.020 --> 00:25:40.820 align:middle line:84%
we can be really good
at randomization.

00:25:40.820 --> 00:25:44.962 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine that if you're
randomizing across whole schools

00:25:44.962 --> 00:25:47.420 align:middle line:84%
or something like that, there
might be people who are like,

00:25:47.420 --> 00:25:49.003 align:middle line:84%
well, I don't want
my school to do it,

00:25:49.003 --> 00:25:50.378 align:middle line:84%
or you put me in
a control group,

00:25:50.378 --> 00:25:52.378 align:middle line:84%
but I just want to get
the intervention, anyway,

00:25:52.378 --> 00:25:53.470 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:25:53.470 --> 00:25:57.580 align:middle line:84%
These kind of digital
systems can be very strict

00:25:57.580 --> 00:25:59.920 align:middle line:84%
about the implementation
of randomization.

00:25:59.920 --> 00:26:03.280 align:middle line:84%
And you can be pretty
sure that there's not

00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:05.218 align:middle line:90%
going to be any kind of bias.

00:26:05.218 --> 00:26:07.010 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine ways
for bias to show up.

00:26:07.010 --> 00:26:10.190 align:middle line:84%
But for the most part, there's
going to be some computer code

00:26:10.190 --> 00:26:15.670 align:middle line:84%
which is like, if exercise 4
assigned someone a 0 or 1--

00:26:15.670 --> 00:26:17.813 align:middle line:90%
control is 0, experiment is 1--

00:26:17.813 --> 00:26:20.230 align:middle line:84%
and especially if you have
hundreds or thousands of people

00:26:20.230 --> 00:26:21.897 align:middle line:84%
who are participating
in the experiment,

00:26:21.897 --> 00:26:24.820 align:middle line:84%
you can be pretty sure that
those two groups are the same.

00:26:24.820 --> 00:26:27.460 align:middle line:84%
Not only that, but
you can also verify

00:26:27.460 --> 00:26:30.010 align:middle line:84%
that they're the same if you
collect all this information

00:26:30.010 --> 00:26:31.840 align:middle line:90%
about them going into it.

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:35.320 align:middle line:84%
So these things are going
to be pretty good at that.

00:26:35.320 --> 00:26:37.910 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well, I don't know
if there was any consent given,

00:26:37.910 --> 00:26:42.310 align:middle line:84%
but from what I can gather
is that this was just

00:26:42.310 --> 00:26:44.620 align:middle line:84%
done without the
students' knowledge.

00:26:44.620 --> 00:26:45.370 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yes.

00:26:45.370 --> 00:26:47.065 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I
wouldn't necessarily

00:26:47.065 --> 00:26:49.750 align:middle line:84%
like it if I came out
of MIT and graduating,

00:26:49.750 --> 00:26:51.250 align:middle line:84%
and then someone's,
like, OK, here's

00:26:51.250 --> 00:26:54.880 align:middle line:84%
a paper of what we analyzed
during your four years here.

00:26:54.880 --> 00:26:56.920 align:middle line:84%
I don't necessarily
want to know that people

00:26:56.920 --> 00:26:59.470 align:middle line:84%
have been like watching
every movement,

00:26:59.470 --> 00:27:02.770 align:middle line:84%
analyzing certain things
about me psychologically.

00:27:02.770 --> 00:27:03.770 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Why not?

00:27:03.770 --> 00:27:05.437 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It feels
a little creepy.

00:27:05.437 --> 00:27:06.895 align:middle line:84%
It feels like you're
being watched.

00:27:06.895 --> 00:27:08.170 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: It could
feel a little creepy, feels

00:27:08.170 --> 00:27:08.980 align:middle line:90%
like you're being watched.

00:27:08.980 --> 00:27:10.360 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Without any consent,
I can see why this is--

00:27:10.360 --> 00:27:11.810 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:27:11.810 --> 00:27:14.320 align:middle line:84%
Other observations
that people have?

00:27:14.320 --> 00:27:18.310 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Also, calling it a
social-psychological message

00:27:18.310 --> 00:27:21.400 align:middle line:84%
makes it feel like the
experimental group's being

00:27:21.400 --> 00:27:23.560 align:middle line:84%
propagandized or
something like that.

00:27:23.560 --> 00:27:25.720 align:middle line:84%
And so it's like, oh,
they're being experimented

00:27:25.720 --> 00:27:28.137 align:middle line:84%
with these different messagings
to make their brains these

00:27:28.137 --> 00:27:29.095 align:middle line:90%
think different things.

00:27:29.095 --> 00:27:31.390 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: With the
social-psychological messages.

00:27:31.390 --> 00:27:34.330 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It seems like
that specific verbiage can

00:27:34.330 --> 00:27:39.070 align:middle line:84%
be uncomfortable for people
to accept as something

00:27:39.070 --> 00:27:41.328 align:middle line:90%
that is potentially beneficial.

00:27:41.328 --> 00:27:42.120 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:27:42.120 --> 00:27:42.230 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:27:42.230 --> 00:27:43.930 align:middle line:84%
So the reason why
people are doing

00:27:43.930 --> 00:27:45.580 align:middle line:84%
this social-psychological
messaging

00:27:45.580 --> 00:27:48.460 align:middle line:84%
is because maybe
it works, actually.

00:27:48.460 --> 00:27:50.080 align:middle line:84%
And in this
particular case, it's

00:27:50.080 --> 00:27:52.760 align:middle line:84%
not just like, hey, we got
kind of a hunch this works.

00:27:52.760 --> 00:27:54.700 align:middle line:84%
These are like, well,
actually, at this point,

00:27:54.700 --> 00:27:59.000 align:middle line:84%
there is maybe a decade of
research that was totally,

00:27:59.000 --> 00:28:01.540 align:middle line:84%
plausibly suggesting that this
could genuinely help students

00:28:01.540 --> 00:28:03.730 align:middle line:84%
learn and improve
their life outcomes

00:28:03.730 --> 00:28:06.370 align:middle line:84%
and other kinds of
things like that.

00:28:06.370 --> 00:28:09.790 align:middle line:84%
And the way you do that is
through a social-psychological

00:28:09.790 --> 00:28:12.850 align:middle line:84%
intervention, although it's not
clear to me what, in schools, is

00:28:12.850 --> 00:28:14.570 align:middle line:90%
not social-psychological.

00:28:14.570 --> 00:28:16.470 align:middle line:90%


00:28:16.470 --> 00:28:18.220 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was going
to say another thing

00:28:18.220 --> 00:28:22.450 align:middle line:84%
that, as much as we need
the two populations to be

00:28:22.450 --> 00:28:28.150 align:middle line:84%
like just as representative,
are the experimental groups even

00:28:28.150 --> 00:28:30.410 align:middle line:84%
representative of the
general population?

00:28:30.410 --> 00:28:33.340 align:middle line:84%
You have to imagine that
people aren't being forced

00:28:33.340 --> 00:28:36.290 align:middle line:90%
to participate in these things.

00:28:36.290 --> 00:28:38.810 align:middle line:84%
Or maybe, if they are, the
schools that they chose,

00:28:38.810 --> 00:28:41.920 align:middle line:84%
are they actually representative
of the entire population?

00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:43.990 align:middle line:84%
Because there,
obviously, are studies

00:28:43.990 --> 00:28:46.520 align:middle line:84%
where they get
outstanding results,

00:28:46.520 --> 00:28:48.670 align:middle line:84%
but then the population
used is not representative

00:28:48.670 --> 00:28:49.880 align:middle line:90%
of the general population.

00:28:49.880 --> 00:28:52.310 align:middle line:84%
So then, once they apply those,
it doesn't actually work.

00:28:52.310 --> 00:28:53.740 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, good.

00:28:53.740 --> 00:28:55.690 align:middle line:84%
And to some extent,
you might think

00:28:55.690 --> 00:28:58.780 align:middle line:84%
that organizations like
Pearson would have an advantage

00:28:58.780 --> 00:29:01.030 align:middle line:90%
over much smaller companies.

00:29:01.030 --> 00:29:04.720 align:middle line:84%
Because if you have products
that are adopted very, very

00:29:04.720 --> 00:29:07.385 align:middle line:84%
widely across the country in
lots of different contexts,

00:29:07.385 --> 00:29:09.010 align:middle line:84%
you might, for
practical purposes, say,

00:29:09.010 --> 00:29:11.200 align:middle line:84%
let's just pick these
handful of schools

00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:13.610 align:middle line:90%
to be able to test things in.

00:29:13.610 --> 00:29:16.780 align:middle line:84%
But you could say, we're going
to pick these handful of schools

00:29:16.780 --> 00:29:20.180 align:middle line:84%
because these are particularly
representative schools.

00:29:20.180 --> 00:29:23.960 align:middle line:84%
Or there might be systems
that are adopted and laid out

00:29:23.960 --> 00:29:26.900 align:middle line:84%
in such a way that you
say, well, let's just pick

00:29:26.900 --> 00:29:29.240 align:middle line:84%
1% of all students
in any of the schools

00:29:29.240 --> 00:29:32.020 align:middle line:84%
who are using our
system to participate

00:29:32.020 --> 00:29:34.020 align:middle line:84%
in this randomized study
or something like that.

00:29:34.020 --> 00:29:35.562 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I feel
like we talked a lot

00:29:35.562 --> 00:29:40.058 align:middle line:84%
about all of the things that go
into using a software program.

00:29:40.058 --> 00:29:41.600 align:middle line:84%
Because, obviously,
that then entails

00:29:41.600 --> 00:29:44.790 align:middle line:84%
that you have something to
run the software program on.

00:29:44.790 --> 00:29:47.540 align:middle line:84%
So anything online already
comes with its own set

00:29:47.540 --> 00:29:52.520 align:middle line:84%
of biases and things that
affect the types of people

00:29:52.520 --> 00:29:53.628 align:middle line:90%
that are using it.

00:29:53.628 --> 00:29:54.420 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:29:54.420 --> 00:29:55.400 align:middle line:90%
No, I think that's fair.

00:29:55.400 --> 00:29:57.108 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Also, another
thing that I just

00:29:57.108 --> 00:30:03.050 align:middle line:84%
realized in what Dana said was
the population that they're

00:30:03.050 --> 00:30:05.720 align:middle line:84%
testing this on is in
college, so they've already

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:08.270 align:middle line:90%
probably developed a mindset.

00:30:08.270 --> 00:30:10.530 align:middle line:90%
And also, they are in college.

00:30:10.530 --> 00:30:13.760 align:middle line:84%
They probably have already
a good enough mindset

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:15.660 align:middle line:90%
that got them to college.

00:30:15.660 --> 00:30:17.650 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yep, although--

00:30:17.650 --> 00:30:20.150 align:middle line:84%
well, one thing that I really
love about this study, which I

00:30:20.150 --> 00:30:22.230 align:middle line:90%
think--

00:30:22.230 --> 00:30:26.240 align:middle line:84%
so after this
article came out, I

00:30:26.240 --> 00:30:28.410 align:middle line:84%
wrote a bunch of things
which basically said,

00:30:28.410 --> 00:30:30.620 align:middle line:84%
I think this is a
pretty unfair article.

00:30:30.620 --> 00:30:32.360 align:middle line:84%
Here, I'll give you
the case for why--

00:30:32.360 --> 00:30:35.865 align:middle line:84%
I think what Pearson
did here is admirable

00:30:35.865 --> 00:30:37.490 align:middle line:84%
and needs to be done
more, although you

00:30:37.490 --> 00:30:39.140 align:middle line:90%
don't have to agree with me.

00:30:39.140 --> 00:30:43.290 align:middle line:84%
So, number one,
generally speaking,

00:30:43.290 --> 00:30:44.990 align:middle line:84%
there's a body of
research that suggests

00:30:44.990 --> 00:30:46.500 align:middle line:90%
these things are a good idea.

00:30:46.500 --> 00:30:49.220 align:middle line:84%
We don't know, however,
are they a good idea

00:30:49.220 --> 00:30:52.440 align:middle line:84%
in this particular product for
this particular population?

00:30:52.440 --> 00:30:55.310 align:middle line:84%
So I think it makes
more sense to run

00:30:55.310 --> 00:30:57.260 align:middle line:84%
an experiment in this
particular product

00:30:57.260 --> 00:30:59.870 align:middle line:84%
for this particular
population to test the thing

00:30:59.870 --> 00:31:01.350 align:middle line:90%
rather than just roll it out.

00:31:01.350 --> 00:31:03.270 align:middle line:84%
This is an important
counterfactual.

00:31:03.270 --> 00:31:06.080 align:middle line:84%
If Pearson had simply
added these messages

00:31:06.080 --> 00:31:08.000 align:middle line:84%
to everyone in the
product, there never

00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:09.950 align:middle line:84%
would have been an
article about it.

00:31:09.950 --> 00:31:14.090 align:middle line:84%
Nobody would have
ever written like,

00:31:14.090 --> 00:31:16.850 align:middle line:84%
"encouraging messages show
up in software product"

00:31:16.850 --> 00:31:20.450 align:middle line:84%
is not an article that even
the Education Press is going

00:31:20.450 --> 00:31:21.830 align:middle line:90%
to populate.

00:31:21.830 --> 00:31:24.150 align:middle line:84%
Number two, another
counterfactual,

00:31:24.150 --> 00:31:27.800 align:middle line:84%
there is no
non-social-psychological

00:31:27.800 --> 00:31:30.500 align:middle line:90%
response to--

00:31:30.500 --> 00:31:34.190 align:middle line:84%
every mark your teacher
has ever put on your paper

00:31:34.190 --> 00:31:36.020 align:middle line:84%
to indicate whether
you're right or wrong

00:31:36.020 --> 00:31:38.510 align:middle line:84%
is a social-psychological
message.

00:31:38.510 --> 00:31:40.350 align:middle line:84%
If you go on EdX and
get something right,

00:31:40.350 --> 00:31:41.460 align:middle line:84%
you're going to
get a green check.

00:31:41.460 --> 00:31:42.600 align:middle line:84%
If you're going to
get something wrong,

00:31:42.600 --> 00:31:44.150 align:middle line:90%
you're going to get a red X.

00:31:44.150 --> 00:31:46.020 align:middle line:90%
That is a set of messaging.

00:31:46.020 --> 00:31:47.420 align:middle line:90%
If you've ever had a--

00:31:47.420 --> 00:31:50.570 align:middle line:84%
none of us would say that
teachers who say "try harder"

00:31:50.570 --> 00:31:52.110 align:middle line:90%
or "that was great.

00:31:52.110 --> 00:31:55.440 align:middle line:84%
I'm really proud of you," no
one would be like, Ms. Jenkins,

00:31:55.440 --> 00:31:57.500 align:middle line:90%
stop manipulating my children.

00:31:57.500 --> 00:32:02.900 align:middle line:84%
No, that's exactly what
we want them to do.

00:32:02.900 --> 00:32:07.070 align:middle line:84%
Some people are
concerned about consent.

00:32:07.070 --> 00:32:10.160 align:middle line:84%
People who go to
school do not consent

00:32:10.160 --> 00:32:12.110 align:middle line:90%
to their learning experiences.

00:32:12.110 --> 00:32:15.160 align:middle line:84%
One of the things that
we do as a state is

00:32:15.160 --> 00:32:17.460 align:middle line:90%
say, you must learn stuff.

00:32:17.460 --> 00:32:20.070 align:middle line:84%
And, in fact, you must learn
stuff that the state just

00:32:20.070 --> 00:32:23.550 align:middle line:90%
requires you to learn.

00:32:23.550 --> 00:32:26.940 align:middle line:84%
These people didn't get to
decide which Pearson product

00:32:26.940 --> 00:32:30.270 align:middle line:84%
or which computer science
project they chose.

00:32:30.270 --> 00:32:33.300 align:middle line:84%
Why should they get to
decide whether or not

00:32:33.300 --> 00:32:35.770 align:middle line:84%
they get an experimental
version or a control version?

00:32:35.770 --> 00:32:39.475 align:middle line:84%
And, in fact, the US
government, for the most part,

00:32:39.475 --> 00:32:40.600 align:middle line:90%
agrees with this principle.

00:32:40.600 --> 00:32:44.400 align:middle line:84%
So there's a rule called the
Common Rule, which dictates

00:32:44.400 --> 00:32:47.940 align:middle line:90%
how research can be conducted.

00:32:47.940 --> 00:32:49.753 align:middle line:84%
There's a bunch
of policy guidance

00:32:49.753 --> 00:32:50.920 align:middle line:90%
that's associated with that.

00:32:50.920 --> 00:32:53.350 align:middle line:84%
And there's some categories
of research that are exempt.

00:32:53.350 --> 00:32:55.530 align:middle line:84%
The first category of
research that's exempt

00:32:55.530 --> 00:32:58.832 align:middle line:84%
is research about typical
educational practices.

00:32:58.832 --> 00:33:01.290 align:middle line:84%
If you're studying the kinds
of ways people teach and learn

00:33:01.290 --> 00:33:04.830 align:middle line:84%
in the classroom, you are
not held to the same standard

00:33:04.830 --> 00:33:09.120 align:middle line:84%
if you're doing pharmaceutical
research or something like that.

00:33:09.120 --> 00:33:10.780 align:middle line:90%
There are some exceptions.

00:33:10.780 --> 00:33:13.530 align:middle line:84%
If you're doing things
that you could reasonably

00:33:13.530 --> 00:33:17.910 align:middle line:84%
believe in advance
might harm people,

00:33:17.910 --> 00:33:19.990 align:middle line:84%
then you're not exempted
in the same way,

00:33:19.990 --> 00:33:23.583 align:middle line:84%
although you can still
conceivably do the research.

00:33:23.583 --> 00:33:25.500 align:middle line:84%
Another thing that's
important about the study

00:33:25.500 --> 00:33:30.720 align:middle line:84%
is that people talk about
the potential benefits

00:33:30.720 --> 00:33:32.100 align:middle line:90%
of an experiment.

00:33:32.100 --> 00:33:34.180 align:middle line:84%
But experiments can
also harm people.

00:33:34.180 --> 00:33:37.980 align:middle line:84%
It could be that, even if, on
average, this growth messaging

00:33:37.980 --> 00:33:39.670 align:middle line:84%
was great and worked
in lots of studies,

00:33:39.670 --> 00:33:41.970 align:middle line:84%
it could be that, in
this particular context

00:33:41.970 --> 00:33:45.687 align:middle line:84%
or other kinds of things,
it made things worse.

00:33:45.687 --> 00:33:48.270 align:middle line:84%
And we should probably make sure
that when we introduce things

00:33:48.270 --> 00:33:50.040 align:middle line:84%
in the classroom, we
make things worse.

00:33:50.040 --> 00:33:52.380 align:middle line:84%
How many people would have
been concerned-- let's

00:33:52.380 --> 00:33:58.440 align:middle line:84%
say that in 2017, they had
everyone use one Pearson

00:33:58.440 --> 00:34:01.210 align:middle line:84%
product, maybe, that doesn't
have any growth messaging.

00:34:01.210 --> 00:34:04.650 align:middle line:84%
And in 2018, everyone
uses a Pearson product

00:34:04.650 --> 00:34:07.282 align:middle line:84%
that had the messaging
associated with it.

00:34:07.282 --> 00:34:09.449 align:middle line:84%
Do you think that would
have raised people's concern

00:34:09.449 --> 00:34:12.550 align:middle line:90%
or felt creepy?

00:34:12.550 --> 00:34:13.050 align:middle line:90%
Why not?

00:34:13.050 --> 00:34:14.125 align:middle line:90%
Why does that feel OK?

00:34:14.125 --> 00:34:22.485 align:middle line:90%


00:34:22.485 --> 00:34:23.610 align:middle line:90%
You're like, wait a minute.

00:34:23.610 --> 00:34:25.352 align:middle line:90%
It's the exact same thing.

00:34:25.352 --> 00:34:26.810 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think
there's this idea

00:34:26.810 --> 00:34:31.230 align:middle line:90%
of potential discrimination.

00:34:31.230 --> 00:34:33.060 align:middle line:84%
Hey, why can't I
be part of that?

00:34:33.060 --> 00:34:36.500 align:middle line:90%
It's either everybody or nobody.

00:34:36.500 --> 00:34:38.750 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: The more an
intervention is particularly

00:34:38.750 --> 00:34:41.900 align:middle line:84%
powerful, the more
you're like, it

00:34:41.900 --> 00:34:46.489 align:middle line:84%
feels kind of like if control
group gets this intervention

00:34:46.489 --> 00:34:51.560 align:middle line:84%
and gets an average grade of
89 and experimental group gets

00:34:51.560 --> 00:34:54.949 align:middle line:84%
the intervention, and they
have an average grade of 89.5,

00:34:54.949 --> 00:34:57.360 align:middle line:84%
there's something that
seems unfair about that,

00:34:57.360 --> 00:35:02.720 align:middle line:84%
although, again, if, in
2018, we give you a software

00:35:02.720 --> 00:35:05.295 align:middle line:84%
product that you get an
average grade of an 89,

00:35:05.295 --> 00:35:06.920 align:middle line:84%
and, in the following
year, we give you

00:35:06.920 --> 00:35:10.440 align:middle line:84%
a software product with
an average grade of 89.5--

00:35:10.440 --> 00:35:13.350 align:middle line:84%
your birth year was
pretty random, too.

00:35:13.350 --> 00:35:16.147 align:middle line:84%
And so there's
something about that.

00:35:16.147 --> 00:35:17.730 align:middle line:84%
The case I want to
make is that here's

00:35:17.730 --> 00:35:20.730 align:middle line:90%
another way to think about it.

00:35:20.730 --> 00:35:24.210 align:middle line:84%
Most people would be appalled
by an education system that

00:35:24.210 --> 00:35:26.040 align:middle line:90%
didn't have variation.

00:35:26.040 --> 00:35:28.650 align:middle line:84%
My hunch is all of you
believe that variation

00:35:28.650 --> 00:35:31.150 align:middle line:84%
is an essential part
of educational systems.

00:35:31.150 --> 00:35:36.110 align:middle line:84%
You believe that your teachers,
your textbook publishers,

00:35:36.110 --> 00:35:37.860 align:middle line:84%
your software developers,
should be trying

00:35:37.860 --> 00:35:39.285 align:middle line:90%
to improve their products.

00:35:39.285 --> 00:35:41.410 align:middle line:84%
They should be trying to
improve their instruction.

00:35:41.410 --> 00:35:43.210 align:middle line:84%
They should be trying to
improve their lesson plans.

00:35:43.210 --> 00:35:45.190 align:middle line:84%
The only way you do that
is through variation.

00:35:45.190 --> 00:35:46.720 align:middle line:84%
You teach one lesson
plan one year.

00:35:46.720 --> 00:35:48.790 align:middle line:84%
You teach a different
lesson plan the next year.

00:35:48.790 --> 00:35:49.540 align:middle line:90%
Sometimes it's better.

00:35:49.540 --> 00:35:50.440 align:middle line:90%
Sometimes it's worse.

00:35:50.440 --> 00:35:53.670 align:middle line:84%
My hunch is you all are totally
comfortable with the idea

00:35:53.670 --> 00:35:57.220 align:middle line:84%
that some of that variation
will be bad and that most of it,

00:35:57.220 --> 00:35:58.740 align:middle line:90%
on average, will be good.

00:35:58.740 --> 00:36:03.060 align:middle line:84%
The main thing that
randomized trials like this do

00:36:03.060 --> 00:36:06.690 align:middle line:84%
is they take that variation,
and they make it systematic

00:36:06.690 --> 00:36:08.340 align:middle line:90%
so we can learn from it.

00:36:08.340 --> 00:36:13.120 align:middle line:84%
If we do one thing in 2017
and a different thing in 2018,

00:36:13.120 --> 00:36:16.050 align:middle line:84%
it's actually much, much
harder to figure out

00:36:16.050 --> 00:36:17.940 align:middle line:84%
if the different
thing worked or not

00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:20.118 align:middle line:84%
because all the
people in 2017 might

00:36:20.118 --> 00:36:21.660 align:middle line:84%
be, in some systematic
way, different

00:36:21.660 --> 00:36:23.370 align:middle line:90%
from the people in 2018.

00:36:23.370 --> 00:36:27.630 align:middle line:84%
The best way to be sure that the
difference between two groups

00:36:27.630 --> 00:36:30.250 align:middle line:84%
is just because of the thing
we're intervening about

00:36:30.250 --> 00:36:31.440 align:middle line:90%
is a randomized trial.

00:36:31.440 --> 00:36:33.735 align:middle line:84%
That is the magic
of randomized trial.

00:36:33.735 --> 00:36:36.390 align:middle line:90%


00:36:36.390 --> 00:36:38.830 align:middle line:84%
Everyone in the education
system believes in variation.

00:36:38.830 --> 00:36:42.420 align:middle line:90%
We all want things to improve.

00:36:42.420 --> 00:36:45.150 align:middle line:84%
It's actually not that
hard to convince people

00:36:45.150 --> 00:36:48.090 align:middle line:84%
that the best way
to systematically

00:36:48.090 --> 00:36:50.700 align:middle line:84%
vary things is
through randomization.

00:36:50.700 --> 00:36:55.980 align:middle line:84%
And almost all of us find
that randomization creepy.

00:36:55.980 --> 00:36:58.033 align:middle line:84%
Even if you explain all
of it, you're like, ah,

00:36:58.033 --> 00:36:59.200 align:middle line:90%
it still doesn't feel right.

00:36:59.200 --> 00:36:59.820 align:middle line:90%
It still doesn't feel--

00:36:59.820 --> 00:37:01.780 align:middle line:84%
there's been some cool studies
they've done in the past.

00:37:01.780 --> 00:37:02.800 align:middle line:90%
It shows up in health care.

00:37:02.800 --> 00:37:04.350 align:middle line:84%
It shows up in other
kinds of things,

00:37:04.350 --> 00:37:08.970 align:middle line:84%
like you ask
patients like, do you

00:37:08.970 --> 00:37:11.680 align:middle line:84%
want an unproven treatment
that might work better?

00:37:11.680 --> 00:37:13.663 align:middle line:84%
And they'll be like,
well, yeah, maybe.

00:37:13.663 --> 00:37:16.080 align:middle line:84%
Do you want to be randomized
to an unproven treatment that

00:37:16.080 --> 00:37:16.830 align:middle line:90%
might work better?

00:37:16.830 --> 00:37:18.870 align:middle line:84%
No, definitely--
and it's like, it's

00:37:18.870 --> 00:37:23.250 align:middle line:84%
the same thing, except we can
learn from the randomization.

00:37:23.250 --> 00:37:26.460 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: My question
is, in this case,

00:37:26.460 --> 00:37:34.890 align:middle line:84%
how are these different from
when companies release betas

00:37:34.890 --> 00:37:37.480 align:middle line:84%
that some people get
and some people don't?

00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:38.710 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Oh, here's--

00:37:38.710 --> 00:37:42.810 align:middle line:84%
every time you interact with
a software product, every time

00:37:42.810 --> 00:37:44.772 align:middle line:84%
you interact with a
web-based software product,

00:37:44.772 --> 00:37:46.230 align:middle line:84%
there is a reasonable
chance you're

00:37:46.230 --> 00:37:49.530 align:middle line:84%
participating in an
experiment just like this.

00:37:49.530 --> 00:37:50.740 align:middle line:90%
The numbers won't be right.

00:37:50.740 --> 00:37:53.340 align:middle line:84%
One out of every 100
times you go to Amazon,

00:37:53.340 --> 00:37:56.320 align:middle line:84%
the buy button is going to
be green instead of blue.

00:37:56.320 --> 00:37:59.070 align:middle line:84%
And someone is doing a test
to figure out whether or not

00:37:59.070 --> 00:38:01.830 align:middle line:84%
you pushing the green
button is better.

00:38:01.830 --> 00:38:05.230 align:middle line:84%
One out of every 100 times, or
1,000 times or 10 times you go

00:38:05.230 --> 00:38:07.990 align:middle line:84%
to Google, they're going to give
you some variation of the search

00:38:07.990 --> 00:38:11.020 align:middle line:84%
algorithm to see if you're
happier or you linger longer

00:38:11.020 --> 00:38:12.820 align:middle line:84%
or you're more
likely to click ads.

00:38:12.820 --> 00:38:17.530 align:middle line:84%
We interact every day
with software platforms

00:38:17.530 --> 00:38:20.290 align:middle line:84%
that are collecting vast
amounts of information on us

00:38:20.290 --> 00:38:23.010 align:middle line:84%
and conducting experiments
on us all the time.

00:38:23.010 --> 00:38:25.600 align:middle line:90%


00:38:25.600 --> 00:38:29.500 align:middle line:84%
The reason why those companies
use those two approaches

00:38:29.500 --> 00:38:33.460 align:middle line:84%
is because they are enormously
powerful at improving products.

00:38:33.460 --> 00:38:37.010 align:middle line:84%
The reason why just about
any query you have--

00:38:37.010 --> 00:38:39.200 align:middle line:84%
you go into Google
and ask it a question,

00:38:39.200 --> 00:38:40.698 align:middle line:84%
and it tells you
an accurate answer

00:38:40.698 --> 00:38:42.490 align:middle line:84%
that's relevant to the
question you asked--

00:38:42.490 --> 00:38:46.900 align:middle line:84%
is because they have conducted
billions of experiments

00:38:46.900 --> 00:38:49.000 align:middle line:84%
on millions or
billions of people

00:38:49.000 --> 00:38:51.880 align:middle line:84%
all throughout the last
20, 25 years that they've

00:38:51.880 --> 00:38:52.870 align:middle line:90%
been operating.

00:38:52.870 --> 00:38:57.850 align:middle line:84%
It is an essential part of
our software infrastructure.

00:38:57.850 --> 00:39:01.390 align:middle line:90%
And I don't like--

00:39:01.390 --> 00:39:05.880 align:middle line:84%
does the idea that you are
participating in experiments all

00:39:05.880 --> 00:39:08.010 align:middle line:84%
the time when you
go Amazon shopping,

00:39:08.010 --> 00:39:12.480 align:middle line:84%
does that have the same
level of creepiness to you?

00:39:12.480 --> 00:39:15.010 align:middle line:84%
For some reason, it's
like, yeah, Jeff Bezos,

00:39:15.010 --> 00:39:16.030 align:middle line:90%
do whatever you want.

00:39:16.030 --> 00:39:20.410 align:middle line:84%
But we feel differently about
Pearson, for some reason.

00:39:20.410 --> 00:39:23.170 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I have a
question about beyond the,

00:39:23.170 --> 00:39:25.060 align:middle line:84%
is this creepy or
not creepy question,

00:39:25.060 --> 00:39:28.740 align:middle line:84%
but if I have decided
to buy a product,

00:39:28.740 --> 00:39:32.170 align:middle line:84%
like I'm paying to use Amazon
or I'm paying to use Pearson,

00:39:32.170 --> 00:39:34.770 align:middle line:84%
and with that is the
assumption that I

00:39:34.770 --> 00:39:37.680 align:middle line:84%
understand the thing I am buying
because you pitched it to me--

00:39:37.680 --> 00:39:38.280 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: I love this.

00:39:38.280 --> 00:39:40.238 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --in a certain
way and I will get it.

00:39:40.238 --> 00:39:42.060 align:middle line:84%
So then how does
the question of, are

00:39:42.060 --> 00:39:44.730 align:middle line:84%
you allowed or not allowed
to do this go from there?

00:39:44.730 --> 00:39:47.340 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: And it
would be a huge difference

00:39:47.340 --> 00:39:51.240 align:middle line:84%
from historical educational
curriculum material purchases.

00:39:51.240 --> 00:39:56.620 align:middle line:84%
It used to be I bought Houghton
Mifflin Biology, eighth edition,

00:39:56.620 --> 00:39:59.470 align:middle line:84%
and I just know exactly
what's in eighth edition.

00:39:59.470 --> 00:40:01.522 align:middle line:84%
And, in fact, the
salesperson is going

00:40:01.522 --> 00:40:03.730 align:middle line:84%
to come back when ninth
edition is going to come out.

00:40:03.730 --> 00:40:04.990 align:middle line:84%
And they're going
to be like, here's

00:40:04.990 --> 00:40:07.220 align:middle line:84%
what's different about ninth
edition, why you could buy it.

00:40:07.220 --> 00:40:08.762 align:middle line:84%
And I might be like,
no, I don't like

00:40:08.762 --> 00:40:10.060 align:middle line:90%
what you did in ninth edition.

00:40:10.060 --> 00:40:12.268 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to switch to a
different company's products

00:40:12.268 --> 00:40:16.990 align:middle line:84%
because these improvements
are not improvements.

00:40:16.990 --> 00:40:19.510 align:middle line:84%
Under what circumstances
should companies

00:40:19.510 --> 00:40:23.410 align:middle line:84%
be able to dynamically improve
their educational products?

00:40:23.410 --> 00:40:27.940 align:middle line:84%
What kind of information should
they make accessible about that?

00:40:27.940 --> 00:40:30.230 align:middle line:90%
Should they tell families?

00:40:30.230 --> 00:40:32.120 align:middle line:90%
Should they tell educators?

00:40:32.120 --> 00:40:35.050 align:middle line:84%
Should they tell-- people could
come up with different answers

00:40:35.050 --> 00:40:36.760 align:middle line:90%
to these questions.

00:40:36.760 --> 00:40:38.440 align:middle line:84%
I think actually
publishing a bunch

00:40:38.440 --> 00:40:41.270 align:middle line:84%
of that information is
probably enormously important

00:40:41.270 --> 00:40:43.923 align:middle line:84%
but not to parents and
teachers and educators.

00:40:43.923 --> 00:40:45.340 align:middle line:84%
Parents and teachers
and educators

00:40:45.340 --> 00:40:49.870 align:middle line:84%
have no time to be tracking how
your Pearson computer science

00:40:49.870 --> 00:40:51.910 align:middle line:90%
practice problems are changing.

00:40:51.910 --> 00:40:55.240 align:middle line:84%
There are specialized advocates
across the country who

00:40:55.240 --> 00:40:57.820 align:middle line:84%
focus on education, who
focus on student privacy

00:40:57.820 --> 00:40:59.608 align:middle line:84%
or other kinds of
things, who actually

00:40:59.608 --> 00:41:01.900 align:middle line:84%
would have the time and
expertise to follow those audit

00:41:01.900 --> 00:41:03.040 align:middle line:90%
records.

00:41:03.040 --> 00:41:05.860 align:middle line:84%
But if we started
sending home a notice

00:41:05.860 --> 00:41:09.370 align:middle line:84%
to parents every time a company
who used one of their products

00:41:09.370 --> 00:41:12.190 align:middle line:84%
was conducting an
experiment, it would just

00:41:12.190 --> 00:41:16.240 align:middle line:84%
be the terms and service
conditions of your--

00:41:16.240 --> 00:41:19.060 align:middle line:84%
presumably, too,
by the way, it's

00:41:19.060 --> 00:41:23.800 align:middle line:84%
very unlikely that when whatever
school bought this Pearson

00:41:23.800 --> 00:41:27.190 align:middle line:84%
product, presumably their
lawyers are competent,

00:41:27.190 --> 00:41:29.650 align:middle line:84%
and they made them sign a
purchase and sale agreement that

00:41:29.650 --> 00:41:31.570 align:middle line:84%
included these
kinds of conditions

00:41:31.570 --> 00:41:35.590 align:middle line:84%
in them, although it's also the
case that the researchers who

00:41:35.590 --> 00:41:38.750 align:middle line:84%
did this thing didn't sit down
with these schools and are like,

00:41:38.750 --> 00:41:41.450 align:middle line:84%
let me tell you what we're doing
in the next phase so that you--

00:41:41.450 --> 00:41:41.950 align:middle line:90%
they might.

00:41:41.950 --> 00:41:43.783 align:middle line:84%
I think, in this case,
they didn't give them

00:41:43.783 --> 00:41:44.630 align:middle line:90%
much indication.

00:41:44.630 --> 00:41:48.340 align:middle line:90%


00:41:48.340 --> 00:41:50.835 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Would it be--

00:41:50.835 --> 00:41:52.210 align:middle line:84%
I guess, back to
the conversation

00:41:52.210 --> 00:41:56.800 align:middle line:84%
of if it's creepy
or not, do you think

00:41:56.800 --> 00:42:00.580 align:middle line:84%
it might have anything to do
with the illusion of choice

00:42:00.580 --> 00:42:01.370 align:middle line:90%
that you're given?

00:42:01.370 --> 00:42:05.260 align:middle line:84%
For example, if you're
on Amazon or something,

00:42:05.260 --> 00:42:08.980 align:middle line:84%
or if you're on an app, you
have the choice sometimes

00:42:08.980 --> 00:42:12.760 align:middle line:84%
not to update the app and just
stay on the previous setting,

00:42:12.760 --> 00:42:18.100 align:middle line:84%
or if you have something like
Amazon, you can not use Amazon,

00:42:18.100 --> 00:42:21.640 align:middle line:84%
or you're perceiving
it as you're

00:42:21.640 --> 00:42:25.840 align:middle line:84%
choosing to opt into that, while
in a situation where you have

00:42:25.840 --> 00:42:28.030 align:middle line:84%
to use a certain
technology in school,

00:42:28.030 --> 00:42:30.970 align:middle line:84%
you could argue that this
student doesn't have a choice

00:42:30.970 --> 00:42:34.672 align:middle line:90%
to opt out of this if they--

00:42:34.672 --> 00:42:36.880 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: And you could
see that choice operating

00:42:36.880 --> 00:42:37.713 align:middle line:90%
at different levels.

00:42:37.713 --> 00:42:40.480 align:middle line:84%
So a grade school student
has zero choice about whether

00:42:40.480 --> 00:42:41.840 align:middle line:90%
or not to go to grade school.

00:42:41.840 --> 00:42:45.010 align:middle line:84%
Every college student
chose to go to college.

00:42:45.010 --> 00:42:48.560 align:middle line:84%
Now, once you've chosen
to go to college,

00:42:48.560 --> 00:42:50.770 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't feel like
that should mean

00:42:50.770 --> 00:42:53.860 align:middle line:84%
you have to accept
everything that comes along

00:42:53.860 --> 00:42:59.590 align:middle line:84%
with college,
although, again, you

00:42:59.590 --> 00:43:01.930 align:middle line:84%
could choose some
majors over other majors

00:43:01.930 --> 00:43:03.350 align:middle line:90%
based on the technology.

00:43:03.350 --> 00:43:05.710 align:middle line:84%
You choose some
classes over classes.

00:43:05.710 --> 00:43:09.310 align:middle line:84%
The moment recently in
which some of this most

00:43:09.310 --> 00:43:14.740 align:middle line:84%
came into focus was when schools
started requiring students

00:43:14.740 --> 00:43:18.550 align:middle line:90%
to adopt proctoring software.

00:43:18.550 --> 00:43:21.010 align:middle line:90%
So probably none of you--

00:43:21.010 --> 00:43:24.370 align:middle line:84%
have any of you ever taken
a remotely proctored test?

00:43:24.370 --> 00:43:25.385 align:middle line:90%
At MIT?

00:43:25.385 --> 00:43:26.260 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:43:26.260 --> 00:43:28.677 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Where did you
take a remotely proctored test

00:43:28.677 --> 00:43:29.290 align:middle line:90%
at MIT?

00:43:29.290 --> 00:43:35.590 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I believe it was
at the ASE during my freshman

00:43:35.590 --> 00:43:36.620 align:middle line:90%
starting year.

00:43:36.620 --> 00:43:39.920 align:middle line:84%
They used some weird
proctoring software for that.

00:43:39.920 --> 00:43:44.210 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Where you had
to take your laptop or webcam,

00:43:44.210 --> 00:43:46.650 align:middle line:84%
show the rest of your room,
all these kinds of things?

00:43:46.650 --> 00:43:47.380 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, essentially.

00:43:47.380 --> 00:43:48.190 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:43:48.190 --> 00:43:52.660 align:middle line:84%
So there are a number
of cases where,

00:43:52.660 --> 00:43:56.230 align:middle line:84%
for instance,
universities implemented

00:43:56.230 --> 00:44:00.200 align:middle line:84%
this kind of proctoring software
mid-semester and, in fact,

00:44:00.200 --> 00:44:03.920 align:middle line:84%
implemented at mid-semester and
required students to pay for it.

00:44:03.920 --> 00:44:07.570 align:middle line:84%
You can imagine that as a set of
circumstances which most draws

00:44:07.570 --> 00:44:10.217 align:middle line:84%
the ire of people
because I've already

00:44:10.217 --> 00:44:11.300 align:middle line:90%
committed to this college.

00:44:11.300 --> 00:44:12.890 align:middle line:84%
I've already committed
to this course.

00:44:12.890 --> 00:44:15.880 align:middle line:84%
Now you're making me,
essentially, install spyware

00:44:15.880 --> 00:44:19.930 align:middle line:84%
on my machine and then, I don't
know, have some random person

00:44:19.930 --> 00:44:22.430 align:middle line:84%
see what my dorm room looks
like and other kinds of things--

00:44:22.430 --> 00:44:23.150 align:middle line:90%
or my bedroom.

00:44:23.150 --> 00:44:25.390 align:middle line:84%
These sort of
things seem creepy.

00:44:25.390 --> 00:44:28.270 align:middle line:84%
Arguably, maybe, they're
less creepy the more that

00:44:28.270 --> 00:44:30.740 align:middle line:84%
they're disclosed
to people over time,

00:44:30.740 --> 00:44:33.940 align:middle line:84%
although I also use the
phrase "illusion of choice"

00:44:33.940 --> 00:44:35.920 align:middle line:84%
because, in fact,
for lots of things--

00:44:35.920 --> 00:44:40.060 align:middle line:84%
for instance, there are
no cell service companies

00:44:40.060 --> 00:44:44.560 align:middle line:84%
that compete on the basis of
not selling your location data.

00:44:44.560 --> 00:44:49.330 align:middle line:84%
It is not really a choice to
not have a mobile phone anymore

00:44:49.330 --> 00:44:50.330 align:middle line:90%
in society.

00:44:50.330 --> 00:44:53.113 align:middle line:90%
And so you're not really--

00:44:53.113 --> 00:44:55.280 align:middle line:84%
in theory, that's a choice,
but it's not really one.

00:44:55.280 --> 00:44:56.580 align:middle line:84%
Going to college
for some people,

00:44:56.580 --> 00:44:57.300 align:middle line:90%
it's not really a choice.

00:44:57.300 --> 00:44:59.092 align:middle line:84%
Like you're going to
have to go to a choice

00:44:59.092 --> 00:45:02.280 align:middle line:84%
to have the life that you
want and things like that.

00:45:02.280 --> 00:45:05.820 align:middle line:84%
But I do think K-12 students,
in particular-- now,

00:45:05.820 --> 00:45:09.030 align:middle line:84%
another unusual feature
of this is that--

00:45:09.030 --> 00:45:13.920 align:middle line:84%
and it goes back to this
sort of data collection--

00:45:13.920 --> 00:45:15.500 align:middle line:84%
many K-12 students
across the country

00:45:15.500 --> 00:45:17.990 align:middle line:84%
are mandated to create
a Google account.

00:45:17.990 --> 00:45:20.030 align:middle line:84%
And then Google
has reams of data

00:45:20.030 --> 00:45:25.190 align:middle line:84%
about all these kinds of things
that they do with that data.

00:45:25.190 --> 00:45:30.260 align:middle line:84%
And there might be
various ways that, again--

00:45:30.260 --> 00:45:33.080 align:middle line:84%
so to what extent these
things are legitimate or not.

00:45:33.080 --> 00:45:35.877 align:middle line:84%
It's probably much less
of a debate these days.

00:45:35.877 --> 00:45:37.460 align:middle line:84%
But definitely when
schools were first

00:45:37.460 --> 00:45:40.220 align:middle line:84%
adopting Google
for Education, it

00:45:40.220 --> 00:45:43.820 align:middle line:84%
was not uncommon to hear
that school boards had

00:45:43.820 --> 00:45:45.810 align:middle line:84%
really vigorous
complaints from parents.

00:45:45.810 --> 00:45:48.560 align:middle line:84%
And oftentimes, the
most vigorous complaints

00:45:48.560 --> 00:45:51.685 align:middle line:84%
came from parents who worked
in the software industry.

00:45:51.685 --> 00:45:53.850 align:middle line:84%
They're like, I know
who these people are.

00:45:53.850 --> 00:45:55.340 align:middle line:90%
I work for these companies.

00:45:55.340 --> 00:45:58.670 align:middle line:84%
How are you going to make my
kid do these kinds of things?

00:45:58.670 --> 00:46:02.570 align:middle line:84%
Let's talk about another
one of these cases.

00:46:02.570 --> 00:46:07.770 align:middle line:84%
So another story that you read
about was inBloom in 2013.

00:46:07.770 --> 00:46:10.640 align:middle line:84%
So the Gates Foundation,
along with a number

00:46:10.640 --> 00:46:11.640 align:middle line:90%
of other organizations--

00:46:11.640 --> 00:46:13.307 align:middle line:84%
Wireless Generation,
some other people--

00:46:13.307 --> 00:46:17.600 align:middle line:84%
observed, man,
here's a weird thing.

00:46:17.600 --> 00:46:24.600 align:middle line:84%
Companies who serve schools have
tons of data about students.

00:46:24.600 --> 00:46:28.460 align:middle line:84%
And the only way we can
make this system work,

00:46:28.460 --> 00:46:30.180 align:middle line:84%
or the way the system
currently works,

00:46:30.180 --> 00:46:33.732 align:middle line:84%
is some company goes to a
school district and says,

00:46:33.732 --> 00:46:35.190 align:middle line:84%
we'll operate this
service for you,

00:46:35.190 --> 00:46:36.920 align:middle line:90%
but here's all the data we need.

00:46:36.920 --> 00:46:40.850 align:middle line:84%
And the schools are
like, yeah, OK, deal.

00:46:40.850 --> 00:46:44.300 align:middle line:84%
And if you make enough of
those kinds of arrangements--

00:46:44.300 --> 00:46:45.830 align:middle line:84%
another exercise
we could do here

00:46:45.830 --> 00:46:49.130 align:middle line:84%
is make a list of all of the
educational software products

00:46:49.130 --> 00:46:51.200 align:middle line:84%
that you've ever
used in schools.

00:46:51.200 --> 00:46:52.670 align:middle line:84%
And you all could
probably come up

00:46:52.670 --> 00:46:55.977 align:middle line:84%
with lists of dozens or
more software products

00:46:55.977 --> 00:46:57.810 align:middle line:84%
that you've encountered
in some kind of way.

00:46:57.810 --> 00:46:59.810 align:middle line:84%
Those are all people that
have some kind of data

00:46:59.810 --> 00:47:01.190 align:middle line:90%
about you somewhere.

00:47:01.190 --> 00:47:03.290 align:middle line:84%
That seems like a
terrible infrastructure.

00:47:03.290 --> 00:47:05.570 align:middle line:84%
You require people
to go to schools.

00:47:05.570 --> 00:47:08.210 align:middle line:84%
The schools make contracts
with third parties

00:47:08.210 --> 00:47:09.480 align:middle line:90%
to provide services.

00:47:09.480 --> 00:47:12.560 align:middle line:84%
And all those third parties
end up with shards of data.

00:47:12.560 --> 00:47:17.030 align:middle line:84%
So the idea of
inBloom was, let's

00:47:17.030 --> 00:47:20.420 align:middle line:84%
concentrate all of the
data about students

00:47:20.420 --> 00:47:25.328 align:middle line:90%
in one nonprofit organization.

00:47:25.328 --> 00:47:26.870 align:middle line:84%
It would be something
along the lines

00:47:26.870 --> 00:47:28.970 align:middle line:84%
of trying to generate
the kind of data

00:47:28.970 --> 00:47:31.190 align:middle line:84%
set that the Norwegian
study that I just showed you

00:47:31.190 --> 00:47:32.480 align:middle line:90%
would require.

00:47:32.480 --> 00:47:36.050 align:middle line:84%
Let's get all their
student records.

00:47:36.050 --> 00:47:38.190 align:middle line:90%
Let's get all their app usage.

00:47:38.190 --> 00:47:39.980 align:middle line:84%
Let's get all those
kinds of things

00:47:39.980 --> 00:47:45.380 align:middle line:84%
and have a nonprofit entity
hold on to that data.

00:47:45.380 --> 00:47:47.600 align:middle line:84%
And, in particular,
let's make it

00:47:47.600 --> 00:47:53.663 align:middle line:84%
so that, instead of
companies making arrangements

00:47:53.663 --> 00:47:55.580 align:middle line:84%
with individual school
districts to get access

00:47:55.580 --> 00:47:57.800 align:middle line:84%
to the data they
need, they just get

00:47:57.800 --> 00:48:00.860 align:middle line:90%
in touch with this nonprofit.

00:48:00.860 --> 00:48:03.770 align:middle line:84%
Now, what could potentially
be good about having

00:48:03.770 --> 00:48:08.443 align:middle line:84%
a nonprofit intermediary between
schools and third parties

00:48:08.443 --> 00:48:10.110 align:middle line:84%
that want to provide
services to schools

00:48:10.110 --> 00:48:11.305 align:middle line:90%
but require data to do it?

00:48:11.305 --> 00:48:14.210 align:middle line:90%


00:48:14.210 --> 00:48:16.460 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think public
perception is that nonprofits

00:48:16.460 --> 00:48:19.170 align:middle line:84%
are a lot more trustworthy
than for-profit companies,

00:48:19.170 --> 00:48:21.090 align:middle line:84%
so people won't get as
freaked out about it.

00:48:21.090 --> 00:48:21.600 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, good.

00:48:21.600 --> 00:48:24.000 align:middle line:84%
So do you want Pearson
to have all of this data,

00:48:24.000 --> 00:48:28.250 align:middle line:84%
or do you want inBloom, funded
by a consortium of state

00:48:28.250 --> 00:48:31.080 align:middle line:84%
departments-- it was funded
by the Gates Foundation

00:48:31.080 --> 00:48:33.540 align:middle line:84%
but sponsored by a set of
state departments of education

00:48:33.540 --> 00:48:34.290 align:middle line:90%
to have this data?

00:48:34.290 --> 00:48:34.790 align:middle line:90%
What else?

00:48:34.790 --> 00:48:38.630 align:middle line:90%


00:48:38.630 --> 00:48:40.860 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I had a question
of how it functions.

00:48:40.860 --> 00:48:44.840 align:middle line:84%
So if they have
the data and then

00:48:44.840 --> 00:48:47.270 align:middle line:84%
a company or some
kind of researcher

00:48:47.270 --> 00:48:50.420 align:middle line:84%
is trying to access it,
do they have final say

00:48:50.420 --> 00:48:52.230 align:middle line:90%
on how it's being used?

00:48:52.230 --> 00:48:55.837 align:middle line:84%
Or do they then go
back to the schools?

00:48:55.837 --> 00:48:57.170 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: So inBloom failed.

00:48:57.170 --> 00:48:58.440 align:middle line:84%
And we can talk
about why it failed.

00:48:58.440 --> 00:48:59.880 align:middle line:84%
So you don't have to
worry about it existing,

00:48:59.880 --> 00:49:01.505 align:middle line:84%
although I'll show
you a new thing that

00:49:01.505 --> 00:49:04.820 align:middle line:84%
showed up this past week,
which is remarkably similar.

00:49:04.820 --> 00:49:08.940 align:middle line:84%
But I think some of the
key value proposition

00:49:08.940 --> 00:49:12.980 align:middle line:84%
was that the companies
would go to inBloom and get

00:49:12.980 --> 00:49:14.432 align:middle line:90%
just the data they need.

00:49:14.432 --> 00:49:16.640 align:middle line:84%
What do you think inBloom
would potentially be better

00:49:16.640 --> 00:49:19.030 align:middle line:90%
at than schools might be?

00:49:19.030 --> 00:49:23.420 align:middle line:90%


00:49:23.420 --> 00:49:24.930 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Raw data processing?

00:49:24.930 --> 00:49:25.722 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:49:25.722 --> 00:49:27.680 align:middle line:84%
So first of all,
they're just like,

00:49:27.680 --> 00:49:32.160 align:middle line:84%
your job is not to teach
kindergarten and Mandarin

00:49:32.160 --> 00:49:33.535 align:middle line:84%
but just to process
student data,

00:49:33.535 --> 00:49:34.952 align:middle line:84%
you're probably
going to be better

00:49:34.952 --> 00:49:37.520 align:middle line:84%
at processing student data
than this organization that

00:49:37.520 --> 00:49:40.190 align:middle line:84%
has to have a field
hockey team and run buses

00:49:40.190 --> 00:49:43.052 align:middle line:84%
and other kinds of
things like that.

00:49:43.052 --> 00:49:44.510 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Maybe
security is better,

00:49:44.510 --> 00:49:46.288 align:middle line:90%
too, if that's your main focus.

00:49:46.288 --> 00:49:48.830 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: There are 13,000
school districts in the United

00:49:48.830 --> 00:49:52.840 align:middle line:84%
States of widely varying
capacities in all kinds of IT

00:49:52.840 --> 00:49:53.340 align:middle line:90%
things.

00:49:53.340 --> 00:49:56.150 align:middle line:84%
It seems like an organization
that is just focused on IT

00:49:56.150 --> 00:49:58.250 align:middle line:90%
things is going to be--

00:49:58.250 --> 00:50:01.160 align:middle line:84%
in a lot of ways, if you have
a society where a school has

00:50:01.160 --> 00:50:03.860 align:middle line:84%
to be good at IT things, you may
not be organizing that society

00:50:03.860 --> 00:50:05.180 align:middle line:90%
correctly.

00:50:05.180 --> 00:50:08.030 align:middle line:84%
And so you can find-- if
you go to other countries

00:50:08.030 --> 00:50:13.760 align:middle line:84%
during the pandemic, we had
schools provision laptops

00:50:13.760 --> 00:50:15.990 align:middle line:90%
and hotspots to students.

00:50:15.990 --> 00:50:16.700 align:middle line:90%
Insane.

00:50:16.700 --> 00:50:18.170 align:middle line:84%
There's no reason
to think schools

00:50:18.170 --> 00:50:19.790 align:middle line:90%
should be any good at that.

00:50:19.790 --> 00:50:22.070 align:middle line:84%
There should be other
institutions in society that

00:50:22.070 --> 00:50:23.720 align:middle line:90%
are good at IT procurement.

00:50:23.720 --> 00:50:25.520 align:middle line:90%
It shouldn't be your local--

00:50:25.520 --> 00:50:29.120 align:middle line:84%
sometimes I would compare it to
people in the 19th century when

00:50:29.120 --> 00:50:31.850 align:middle line:84%
we decided that every household
in the United States should

00:50:31.850 --> 00:50:34.257 align:middle line:84%
get-- or in the 20th century--
we decided every household

00:50:34.257 --> 00:50:36.090 align:middle line:84%
in the United States
should get electricity.

00:50:36.090 --> 00:50:38.180 align:middle line:84%
We didn't go to the
local school principal

00:50:38.180 --> 00:50:39.555 align:middle line:84%
and be like, so
how are you going

00:50:39.555 --> 00:50:41.330 align:middle line:90%
to run wires up the holler?

00:50:41.330 --> 00:50:44.270 align:middle line:84%
We invented the TVA and
other kinds of organizations

00:50:44.270 --> 00:50:46.567 align:middle line:84%
that was really good
at electrification

00:50:46.567 --> 00:50:49.150 align:middle line:84%
so that the schools didn't have
to be good at electrification.

00:50:49.150 --> 00:50:54.040 align:middle line:84%
Electrification is closer
to provisioning broadband

00:50:54.040 --> 00:50:57.980 align:middle line:84%
and laptops than it is to other
kinds of educational things.

00:50:57.980 --> 00:51:00.640 align:middle line:84%
So these institutions
could conceivably

00:51:00.640 --> 00:51:02.750 align:middle line:84%
develop a whole
bunch of expertise

00:51:02.750 --> 00:51:05.837 align:middle line:84%
around data, around
privacy, things like that.

00:51:05.837 --> 00:51:07.420 align:middle line:84%
Another thing that
they could probably

00:51:07.420 --> 00:51:11.380 align:middle line:84%
get pretty good at doing
is only giving providers

00:51:11.380 --> 00:51:13.450 align:middle line:90%
the exact data they need.

00:51:13.450 --> 00:51:16.025 align:middle line:84%
So schools have a tough
time vetting all of the data

00:51:16.025 --> 00:51:18.650 align:middle line:84%
that they're making available to
all their different providers.

00:51:18.650 --> 00:51:20.800 align:middle line:84%
And so, presumably,
they make arrangements,

00:51:20.800 --> 00:51:22.280 align:middle line:84%
and people just
collect whatever.

00:51:22.280 --> 00:51:25.600 align:middle line:84%
If there's a intermediary
in that collection process,

00:51:25.600 --> 00:51:27.100 align:middle line:84%
you could imagine
that inBloom could

00:51:27.100 --> 00:51:29.360 align:middle line:84%
be pretty good at being
like, yeah, Canvas,

00:51:29.360 --> 00:51:31.010 align:middle line:84%
you just asked us
for these 14 things,

00:51:31.010 --> 00:51:33.838 align:middle line:84%
but I'm pretty sure, to operate
your system, you need these six.

00:51:33.838 --> 00:51:35.380 align:middle line:84%
So why don't you
start with these six

00:51:35.380 --> 00:51:37.120 align:middle line:90%
and see how you do from there?

00:51:37.120 --> 00:51:39.520 align:middle line:84%
And they might be able to do
all kinds of other-- right

00:51:39.520 --> 00:51:41.050 align:middle line:84%
now, for Canvas to
operate, it needs

00:51:41.050 --> 00:51:44.987 align:middle line:84%
to have your actual email
address and other kinds

00:51:44.987 --> 00:51:45.820 align:middle line:90%
of things like that.

00:51:45.820 --> 00:51:48.278 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine building all
kinds of intermediary layers

00:51:48.278 --> 00:51:50.400 align:middle line:84%
that only hashed
identifiers are passing

00:51:50.400 --> 00:51:55.230 align:middle line:84%
on to these third parties,
and inBloom collects all this.

00:51:55.230 --> 00:52:00.415 align:middle line:84%
inBloom fails
almost immediately.

00:52:00.415 --> 00:52:02.790 align:middle line:84%
Well, here's a few things that
were written about it when

00:52:02.790 --> 00:52:04.890 align:middle line:90%
it started.

00:52:04.890 --> 00:52:07.380 align:middle line:84%
"Nonprofit inBloom offers
an internet database service

00:52:07.380 --> 00:52:09.090 align:middle line:84%
that allows schools to store,
track, and analyze data

00:52:09.090 --> 00:52:09.790 align:middle line:90%
on school children.

00:52:09.790 --> 00:52:10.920 align:middle line:84%
If you think about
it, that information

00:52:10.920 --> 00:52:12.220 align:middle line:90%
is more than just test scores.

00:52:12.220 --> 00:52:14.190 align:middle line:84%
It's whether kids receive
free lunch, a telling

00:52:14.190 --> 00:52:15.260 align:middle line:84%
indicator of the
family's finances,

00:52:15.260 --> 00:52:17.170 align:middle line:84%
the time the school got into
a fight in the schoolyard.

00:52:17.170 --> 00:52:19.200 align:middle line:84%
It could be your child's
prescription medicine.

00:52:19.200 --> 00:52:21.150 align:middle line:84%
The upshot of storing all
that data in one location

00:52:21.150 --> 00:52:23.350 align:middle line:84%
is that it can be used to
tailor curricula to each child.

00:52:23.350 --> 00:52:25.600 align:middle line:84%
If Johnny's data suggests
he's a tactile learner"--

00:52:25.600 --> 00:52:29.460 align:middle line:84%
which is not true, there
are no learning styles--

00:52:29.460 --> 00:52:31.740 align:middle line:84%
"and he's failing math,
inBloom's analytic action

00:52:31.740 --> 00:52:33.880 align:middle line:84%
might suggest a particular
teaching approach.

00:52:33.880 --> 00:52:35.880 align:middle line:84%
This is all about building
personalized learning

00:52:35.880 --> 00:52:37.950 align:middle line:90%
environments for kids."

00:52:37.950 --> 00:52:40.240 align:middle line:84%
But as more school districts
team up with inBloom,

00:52:40.240 --> 00:52:42.330 align:middle line:84%
including New York, parents
are becoming increasingly vocal

00:52:42.330 --> 00:52:44.140 align:middle line:84%
critics of the data
collection. 'This information,

00:52:44.140 --> 00:52:45.460 align:middle line:90%
I have no idea what it is.

00:52:45.460 --> 00:52:46.780 align:middle line:90%
I have no idea who's using it.

00:52:46.780 --> 00:52:50.550 align:middle line:84%
And I have no idea
for what purpose.'"

00:52:50.550 --> 00:52:54.030 align:middle line:84%
So part of what the
existence of inBloom did

00:52:54.030 --> 00:52:57.400 align:middle line:84%
was to go to communities
and be like, hey, you know

00:52:57.400 --> 00:52:58.870 align:middle line:90%
what's happening right now?

00:52:58.870 --> 00:53:02.040 align:middle line:84%
There's tons of data being
collected about children.

00:53:02.040 --> 00:53:05.250 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to put
it all in one place.

00:53:05.250 --> 00:53:07.613 align:middle line:84%
You can imagine that,
for people like me,

00:53:07.613 --> 00:53:09.280 align:middle line:84%
they're like, yeah,
that's a great idea.

00:53:09.280 --> 00:53:11.110 align:middle line:84%
We're totally going to be able
to have all these benefits.

00:53:11.110 --> 00:53:13.860 align:middle line:84%
But there's tons of data being
collected about children,

00:53:13.860 --> 00:53:16.290 align:middle line:84%
and it's all going to be
put in one place is not,

00:53:16.290 --> 00:53:19.530 align:middle line:84%
actually, as it turns out,
a very popular public pitch.

00:53:19.530 --> 00:53:21.360 align:middle line:84%
"inBloom CEO is
quick to point out

00:53:21.360 --> 00:53:25.440 align:middle line:84%
that the company isn't
collecting data the school

00:53:25.440 --> 00:53:26.835 align:middle line:90%
districts don't already have.

00:53:26.835 --> 00:53:29.460 align:middle line:84%
It's just providing a one-stop
shop to make the data accessible

00:53:29.460 --> 00:53:31.210 align:middle line:84%
and usable to teachers,"
although, really,

00:53:31.210 --> 00:53:33.585 align:middle line:84%
a lot of what it was doing
was making the data accessible

00:53:33.585 --> 00:53:35.820 align:middle line:84%
and usable to third parties,
which might then benefit

00:53:35.820 --> 00:53:36.430 align:middle line:90%
teachers.

00:53:36.430 --> 00:53:38.440 align:middle line:84%
"Schools and districts
collect a lot of data,

00:53:38.440 --> 00:53:40.560 align:middle line:84%
but it's all over the
place, Steinberger says.

00:53:40.560 --> 00:53:42.270 align:middle line:84%
Different systems
are disconnected.

00:53:42.270 --> 00:53:45.580 align:middle line:84%
And so, as a result,
this data is not usable."

00:53:45.580 --> 00:53:47.980 align:middle line:90%
Can anybody think of--

00:53:47.980 --> 00:53:49.920 align:middle line:84%
so that was a very
serious problem.

00:53:49.920 --> 00:53:51.640 align:middle line:84%
Schools are collecting
tons of data.

00:53:51.640 --> 00:53:53.437 align:middle line:84%
It's fragmented across
all these services,

00:53:53.437 --> 00:53:54.520 align:middle line:90%
all these kinds of things.

00:53:54.520 --> 00:53:59.760 align:middle line:84%
And so, in a sense, there's
constant ongoing either privacy

00:53:59.760 --> 00:54:04.990 align:middle line:84%
risk, privacy violations, and
not sensible ways to manage it.

00:54:04.990 --> 00:54:07.240 align:middle line:84%
And that's the problem that
inBloom's trying to solve.

00:54:07.240 --> 00:54:11.210 align:middle line:84%
Can anybody think of, around
2013, major things that

00:54:11.210 --> 00:54:12.960 align:middle line:84%
are happening in the
United States related

00:54:12.960 --> 00:54:18.540 align:middle line:84%
to people's perception of
their data and security?

00:54:18.540 --> 00:54:20.550 align:middle line:84%
You all are a little
young for this now.

00:54:20.550 --> 00:54:22.590 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is that [INAUDIBLE]
Target [INAUDIBLE]

00:54:22.590 --> 00:54:25.173 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Target, I think,
is not that far away from that.

00:54:25.173 --> 00:54:28.560 align:middle line:84%
So we start seeing some of the
major data security breaches.

00:54:28.560 --> 00:54:31.525 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's like
Cambridge Analytica.

00:54:31.525 --> 00:54:33.900 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: I think Cambridge
Analytica is a little bit

00:54:33.900 --> 00:54:36.520 align:middle line:84%
afterwards because that is
around the 2016 election.

00:54:36.520 --> 00:54:37.710 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Snowden.

00:54:37.710 --> 00:54:43.290 align:middle line:84%
This is the Edward
Snowden era of recognizing

00:54:43.290 --> 00:54:44.790 align:middle line:84%
the federal government
is collecting

00:54:44.790 --> 00:54:47.560 align:middle line:84%
much more data about people
than was previously thought.

00:54:47.560 --> 00:54:50.670 align:middle line:84%
So in 2013, first
of all, people are

00:54:50.670 --> 00:54:52.920 align:middle line:84%
far less accustomed
to surveillance

00:54:52.920 --> 00:54:55.740 align:middle line:84%
than you all described yourself
as accustomed to surveillance.

00:54:55.740 --> 00:54:58.860 align:middle line:84%
And there had just been this
major national revelation.

00:54:58.860 --> 00:55:02.040 align:middle line:84%
An interesting thing about
the inBloom story is--

00:55:02.040 --> 00:55:04.530 align:middle line:84%
so, eventually, what happens
is there were 11 states that

00:55:04.530 --> 00:55:05.950 align:middle line:90%
signed on initially.

00:55:05.950 --> 00:55:07.957 align:middle line:84%
And the hope was is
that then 13 and 14

00:55:07.957 --> 00:55:10.290 align:middle line:84%
and 18 and 20, that, eventually,
lots and lots of states

00:55:10.290 --> 00:55:10.960 align:middle line:90%
would come on.

00:55:10.960 --> 00:55:14.130 align:middle line:84%
Almost immediately, states
started withdrawing from it,

00:55:14.130 --> 00:55:20.010 align:middle line:84%
in particular, in part because
of the concentrated objection

00:55:20.010 --> 00:55:24.520 align:middle line:84%
from local advocates,
local advocacy groups,

00:55:24.520 --> 00:55:27.780 align:middle line:84%
including student privacy
groups that were just

00:55:27.780 --> 00:55:28.840 align:middle line:90%
formed around this time.

00:55:28.840 --> 00:55:30.443 align:middle line:84%
New York was one
of the lead ones.

00:55:30.443 --> 00:55:32.110 align:middle line:84%
It was one of the
last ones to fall out.

00:55:32.110 --> 00:55:36.360 align:middle line:84%
But it also had one of the most
vociferous, determined community

00:55:36.360 --> 00:55:38.940 align:middle line:84%
of student privacy
advocates opting.

00:55:38.940 --> 00:55:45.600 align:middle line:84%
So states started-- one of
the main outcomes of inBloom

00:55:45.600 --> 00:55:48.030 align:middle line:84%
was not its existence,
actually, but a bunch of state

00:55:48.030 --> 00:55:49.900 align:middle line:84%
laws about student
privacy, in part,

00:55:49.900 --> 00:55:51.733 align:middle line:84%
that would make it more
difficult for things

00:55:51.733 --> 00:55:53.400 align:middle line:90%
like inBloom to exist.

00:55:53.400 --> 00:55:58.500 align:middle line:84%
A common problem that shows
up when trying to fix data

00:55:58.500 --> 00:56:01.200 align:middle line:84%
and privacy problems-- and
I'm giving you an example from

00:56:01.200 --> 00:56:05.880 align:middle line:84%
schools, but this is a thing
that you can find in almost any

00:56:05.880 --> 00:56:07.290 align:middle line:90%
other sector--

00:56:07.290 --> 00:56:11.710 align:middle line:84%
the pre-inBloom counterfactual
is really, really bad.

00:56:11.710 --> 00:56:15.270 align:middle line:84%
It is not a good system to force
kids to go to public schools

00:56:15.270 --> 00:56:17.400 align:middle line:84%
and then have a bunch of
third-party, for-profit

00:56:17.400 --> 00:56:19.780 align:middle line:84%
companies get their
data from schools.

00:56:19.780 --> 00:56:21.370 align:middle line:84%
The schools are
bad at managing it.

00:56:21.370 --> 00:56:24.450 align:middle line:84%
They don't have the expertise,
all these kinds of things.

00:56:24.450 --> 00:56:28.216 align:middle line:84%
However, by trying
to fix the problem,

00:56:28.216 --> 00:56:31.371 align:middle line:84%
inBloom draws attention
to the problem.

00:56:31.371 --> 00:56:33.270 align:middle line:84%
Like, man, this
data is such a mess,

00:56:33.270 --> 00:56:34.770 align:middle line:84%
and we're going
to collect it all.

00:56:34.770 --> 00:56:36.390 align:middle line:84%
There are all kinds
of circumstances

00:56:36.390 --> 00:56:40.610 align:middle line:84%
in which if you draw attention
to data and experiments,

00:56:40.610 --> 00:56:45.470 align:middle line:84%
even if the status quo
is really terrible,

00:56:45.470 --> 00:56:47.830 align:middle line:84%
people are not going to be
enthusiastic about the fix

00:56:47.830 --> 00:56:50.360 align:middle line:84%
because nobody thinks day
to day about the status quo,

00:56:50.360 --> 00:56:53.440 align:middle line:84%
but they think really intensely
about this crazy inBloom thing

00:56:53.440 --> 00:56:55.420 align:middle line:90%
that you're doing.

00:56:55.420 --> 00:56:57.110 align:middle line:90%
Does that idea make sense?

00:56:57.110 --> 00:57:00.070 align:middle line:84%
Essentially, there's all--
other studies come out

00:57:00.070 --> 00:57:02.950 align:middle line:84%
along the lines of,
hey, we've come up

00:57:02.950 --> 00:57:05.690 align:middle line:84%
with this really good way
to protect your privacy.

00:57:05.690 --> 00:57:06.740 align:middle line:90%
Will you opt into it?

00:57:06.740 --> 00:57:08.980 align:middle line:90%
People will be like, whoa.

00:57:08.980 --> 00:57:11.077 align:middle line:84%
Why would I have to
protect my privacy?

00:57:11.077 --> 00:57:12.410 align:middle line:90%
Why would I opt into this thing?

00:57:12.410 --> 00:57:12.860 align:middle line:90%
It's dangerous.

00:57:12.860 --> 00:57:15.250 align:middle line:84%
They would prefer the status
quo, where their privacy is not

00:57:15.250 --> 00:57:17.170 align:middle line:84%
protected, towards this
thing in which you've

00:57:17.170 --> 00:57:18.447 align:middle line:90%
raised their concerns about.

00:57:18.447 --> 00:57:23.380 align:middle line:90%


00:57:23.380 --> 00:57:28.910 align:middle line:84%
So this is an organization that
just came into life last week.

00:57:28.910 --> 00:57:31.420 align:middle line:90%
It's called SafeInsights.

00:57:31.420 --> 00:57:36.340 align:middle line:84%
It was funded by $90 million
from the National Science

00:57:36.340 --> 00:57:37.420 align:middle line:90%
Foundation.

00:57:37.420 --> 00:57:40.030 align:middle line:84%
It is out of Rice
University, out

00:57:40.030 --> 00:57:41.925 align:middle line:84%
of the same people that
developed OpenStax.

00:57:41.925 --> 00:57:43.550 align:middle line:84%
We talked about
OpenStax the other day.

00:57:43.550 --> 00:57:47.890 align:middle line:84%
They're the company that creates
free and open-source textbooks

00:57:47.890 --> 00:57:49.290 align:middle line:90%
for community college folks.

00:57:49.290 --> 00:57:52.517 align:middle line:90%


00:57:52.517 --> 00:57:53.600 align:middle line:90%
Well, we have enough time.

00:57:53.600 --> 00:57:56.080 align:middle line:90%
I'll show you the one--

00:57:56.080 --> 00:57:59.550 align:middle line:84%
let's see if we can find the
SafeInsights welcome video.

00:57:59.550 --> 00:58:03.350 align:middle line:90%


00:58:03.350 --> 00:58:04.310 align:middle line:90%
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:58:04.310 --> 00:58:07.670 align:middle line:90%
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:58:07.670 --> 00:58:11.530 align:middle line:90%


00:58:11.530 --> 00:58:13.670 align:middle line:84%
- As a longtime
college instructor,

00:58:13.670 --> 00:58:16.870 align:middle line:84%
every day of my career
has been dedicated

00:58:16.870 --> 00:58:20.410 align:middle line:84%
to helping my students
reach their academic goals.

00:58:20.410 --> 00:58:24.130 align:middle line:84%
I'm always looking to better
support their learning in ways

00:58:24.130 --> 00:58:25.970 align:middle line:90%
that are proven to work best.

00:58:25.970 --> 00:58:28.060 align:middle line:84%
- There's so much we
have to understand

00:58:28.060 --> 00:58:29.420 align:middle line:90%
about how people learn.

00:58:29.420 --> 00:58:31.880 align:middle line:84%
Every mind is
beautiful and special.

00:58:31.880 --> 00:58:34.540 align:middle line:84%
That means we all learn
in different ways.

00:58:34.540 --> 00:58:38.150 align:middle line:84%
We all have our own definitions
of what success looks like.

00:58:38.150 --> 00:58:41.530 align:middle line:84%
If researchers are able to
uncover new and better ways

00:58:41.530 --> 00:58:44.710 align:middle line:84%
of learning, that will have
a profound impact on how

00:58:44.710 --> 00:58:46.750 align:middle line:90%
educators approach teaching.

00:58:46.750 --> 00:58:49.690 align:middle line:84%
To do this, we need a
way to securely connect

00:58:49.690 --> 00:58:52.390 align:middle line:84%
with a wide variety of
students as they study

00:58:52.390 --> 00:58:55.420 align:middle line:90%
over time in real classrooms.

00:58:55.420 --> 00:58:57.230 align:middle line:90%
- Student needs are changing.

00:58:57.230 --> 00:58:59.480 align:middle line:84%
With more learning
happening online,

00:58:59.480 --> 00:59:02.260 align:middle line:84%
it gets harder and
harder to understand

00:59:02.260 --> 00:59:06.700 align:middle line:84%
what each student needs and how
best to help them at the moment

00:59:06.700 --> 00:59:08.930 align:middle line:84%
when they really
need it the most.

00:59:08.930 --> 00:59:11.710 align:middle line:84%
- Education research is
essential for advancing teaching

00:59:11.710 --> 00:59:14.390 align:middle line:84%
and learning so that
every student can succeed.

00:59:14.390 --> 00:59:17.210 align:middle line:84%
We know that research-informed
teaching works,

00:59:17.210 --> 00:59:18.850 align:middle line:84%
but it's still too
hard to conduct

00:59:18.850 --> 00:59:21.400 align:middle line:84%
large-scale, reliable
research and then apply

00:59:21.400 --> 00:59:22.910 align:middle line:90%
the results in the classroom.

00:59:22.910 --> 00:59:26.750 align:middle line:84%
SafeInsights brings together
learning researchers, educators,

00:59:26.750 --> 00:59:29.530 align:middle line:84%
schools, and colleges
in a secure environment

00:59:29.530 --> 00:59:33.070 align:middle line:84%
to get the answers we need while
strongly protecting students

00:59:33.070 --> 00:59:34.060 align:middle line:90%
and their interests.

00:59:34.060 --> 00:59:36.250 align:middle line:84%
- Every student
deserves the chance

00:59:36.250 --> 00:59:39.745 align:middle line:84%
to succeed in the
classroom and beyond.

00:59:39.745 --> 00:59:42.290 align:middle line:84%
- SafeInsights will give
researchers, educators,

00:59:42.290 --> 00:59:45.230 align:middle line:84%
and students what they need to
take learning to the next level.

00:59:45.230 --> 00:59:47.380 align:middle line:84%
- And as educators,
we will benefit

00:59:47.380 --> 00:59:50.030 align:middle line:84%
from better research
from SafeInsights

00:59:50.030 --> 00:59:53.456 align:middle line:84%
to effectively help
all our students.

00:59:53.456 --> 00:59:56.372 align:middle line:90%
[DRAMATIC MUSIC]

00:59:56.372 --> 00:59:59.774 align:middle line:90%


00:59:59.774 --> 01:00:01.240 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

01:00:01.240 --> 01:00:03.680 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Great soundtrack.

01:00:03.680 --> 01:00:07.246 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: It is a little
dramatic for a database.

01:00:07.246 --> 01:00:09.980 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHS] But I don't know.

01:00:09.980 --> 01:00:10.700 align:middle line:90%
I like databases.

01:00:10.700 --> 01:00:14.650 align:middle line:84%
OK what are your first
instincts, having

01:00:14.650 --> 01:00:17.170 align:middle line:90%
spent a whole bunch of time--

01:00:17.170 --> 01:00:19.270 align:middle line:84%
having spent an
hour and 20 minutes

01:00:19.270 --> 01:00:21.770 align:middle line:84%
talking about the toxic power
of data and experiments?

01:00:21.770 --> 01:00:24.028 align:middle line:84%
And how do you think a
typical American parent

01:00:24.028 --> 01:00:25.570 align:middle line:84%
or a typical American
college student

01:00:25.570 --> 01:00:27.640 align:middle line:90%
would react to that video?

01:00:27.640 --> 01:00:29.680 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think
it would change

01:00:29.680 --> 01:00:31.070 align:middle line:90%
based on different generations.

01:00:31.070 --> 01:00:33.850 align:middle line:84%
Because I think our
generation, who is now

01:00:33.850 --> 01:00:36.490 align:middle line:84%
using TikTok when we openly
know they use our data

01:00:36.490 --> 01:00:38.500 align:middle line:84%
and [INAUDIBLE] to
us, is completely

01:00:38.500 --> 01:00:42.100 align:middle line:84%
different from our parents,
who are like, oh, my god,

01:00:42.100 --> 01:00:43.402 align:middle line:90%
don't have a profile picture.

01:00:43.402 --> 01:00:44.360 align:middle line:90%
Don't do this and that.

01:00:44.360 --> 01:00:48.470 align:middle line:84%
So I think our generation will
be way more accepting of this.

01:00:48.470 --> 01:00:52.023 align:middle line:84%
And I think, in 10, 20
years down the line,

01:00:52.023 --> 01:00:53.440 align:middle line:84%
I think people and
parents will be

01:00:53.440 --> 01:00:56.960 align:middle line:84%
so accustomed to their
children's data just being,

01:00:56.960 --> 01:00:57.580 align:middle line:90%
whatever.

01:00:57.580 --> 01:00:58.550 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

01:00:58.550 --> 01:01:01.513 align:middle line:84%
So conceivably,
inBloom launched today

01:01:01.513 --> 01:01:04.180 align:middle line:84%
might have had a very different
experience than inBloom launched

01:01:04.180 --> 01:01:06.610 align:middle line:84%
in 2013, not because
of anything different

01:01:06.610 --> 01:01:09.565 align:middle line:84%
that they did but because of the
context that they operate in.

01:01:09.565 --> 01:01:10.940 align:middle line:84%
I think there's
evidence of that.

01:01:10.940 --> 01:01:12.357 align:middle line:84%
I was just talking
to somebody who

01:01:12.357 --> 01:01:16.720 align:middle line:84%
runs a video repository for
child development studies.

01:01:16.720 --> 01:01:21.080 align:middle line:84%
And one of their conditions
is that the videos can only

01:01:21.080 --> 01:01:22.580 align:middle line:84%
be used for
non-commercial purposes.

01:01:22.580 --> 01:01:24.290 align:middle line:84%
And I was like, why
did you do that?

01:01:24.290 --> 01:01:26.000 align:middle line:84%
And they were like, well,
we made it 10 years ago,

01:01:26.000 --> 01:01:27.130 align:middle line:84%
and people were really
worried about stuff,

01:01:27.130 --> 01:01:28.520 align:middle line:90%
and that helped calm them down.

01:01:28.520 --> 01:01:30.380 align:middle line:84%
But if we did it
again today, I don't

01:01:30.380 --> 01:01:33.590 align:middle line:84%
think the same level of concern
exists, not only among people

01:01:33.590 --> 01:01:36.500 align:middle line:84%
generally but
institutionally, as well.

01:01:36.500 --> 01:01:38.042 align:middle line:84%
What were you going
to say, Courtney?

01:01:38.042 --> 01:01:40.125 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think it was
really smart that they've

01:01:40.125 --> 01:01:41.600 align:middle line:90%
attached themselves to Rice.

01:01:41.600 --> 01:01:43.950 align:middle line:84%
Because I think, especially
our parents' generation,

01:01:43.950 --> 01:01:46.100 align:middle line:84%
maybe less so now,
there's a lot of goodwill

01:01:46.100 --> 01:01:47.490 align:middle line:90%
given to universities.

01:01:47.490 --> 01:01:49.760 align:middle line:84%
And so if inBloom came
out today and it was like,

01:01:49.760 --> 01:01:51.720 align:middle line:84%
oh, in partnership
with MIT, I do

01:01:51.720 --> 01:01:53.970 align:middle line:84%
think people would have
received it a lot differently.

01:01:53.970 --> 01:01:56.222 align:middle line:84%
And people have a lot
of trust that, oh,

01:01:56.222 --> 01:01:57.180 align:middle line:90%
it's connected to Rice.

01:01:57.180 --> 01:01:58.260 align:middle line:90%
How bad could it really be?

01:01:58.260 --> 01:01:59.052 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

01:01:59.052 --> 01:02:03.350 align:middle line:84%
And I would say the Gates
Foundation-related conspiracy

01:02:03.350 --> 01:02:06.350 align:middle line:84%
theories were not quite
as strong in 2013.

01:02:06.350 --> 01:02:09.500 align:middle line:84%
But the Gates Foundation is
not a trusted organization

01:02:09.500 --> 01:02:11.180 align:middle line:90%
in the same way that, I think--

01:02:11.180 --> 01:02:14.960 align:middle line:84%
Rice University, universities
in general, state schools,

01:02:14.960 --> 01:02:19.010 align:middle line:84%
are still some of the most
trusted institutions in society.

01:02:19.010 --> 01:02:22.490 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think, maybe,
actually, a little connected

01:02:22.490 --> 01:02:24.000 align:middle line:84%
to that trustworthiness,
I think,

01:02:24.000 --> 01:02:27.860 align:middle line:84%
is that if you say, oh, my
god, look, all your data is

01:02:27.860 --> 01:02:32.720 align:middle line:84%
in one place, to someone
who finds education research

01:02:32.720 --> 01:02:34.400 align:middle line:84%
and things like that
really important

01:02:34.400 --> 01:02:40.620 align:middle line:84%
and is much more aware
of how that might help,

01:02:40.620 --> 01:02:41.760 align:middle line:90%
that might sound good.

01:02:41.760 --> 01:02:44.630 align:middle line:84%
But I think, to the
average person, first

01:02:44.630 --> 01:02:46.430 align:middle line:84%
and foremost, what's
going to pop out

01:02:46.430 --> 01:02:50.150 align:middle line:84%
when you just tell them, oh, we
have everything in one place,

01:02:50.150 --> 01:02:53.090 align:middle line:84%
and we promise it's
safe, it's like, one,

01:02:53.090 --> 01:02:54.620 align:middle line:84%
you're not giving
me any information

01:02:54.620 --> 01:02:56.580 align:middle line:84%
about how you're
keeping the data safe,

01:02:56.580 --> 01:03:00.890 align:middle line:84%
and, two, I think it's
always been said of,

01:03:00.890 --> 01:03:03.330 align:middle line:84%
don't put all your
data in one place.

01:03:03.330 --> 01:03:08.010 align:middle line:84%
So it's like, well, why are you
saying this is a good thing?

01:03:08.010 --> 01:03:12.620 align:middle line:84%
Because then this means that
someone can have all my data,

01:03:12.620 --> 01:03:16.410 align:middle line:84%
as opposed to, let's say, if it
is split up, even if it's there,

01:03:16.410 --> 01:03:19.820 align:middle line:84%
I can be like, OK,
my teacher might

01:03:19.820 --> 01:03:22.210 align:middle line:84%
have this piece of data
on me, but they might not

01:03:22.210 --> 01:03:23.460 align:middle line:90%
have this other piece of data.

01:03:23.460 --> 01:03:25.980 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: And, actually,
probably the main technical--

01:03:25.980 --> 01:03:26.720 align:middle line:90%
there are probably a bunch.

01:03:26.720 --> 01:03:28.000 align:middle line:84%
We don't know exactly
where they'll be then.

01:03:28.000 --> 01:03:30.690 align:middle line:84%
But one of the earliest
proposed technical innovations

01:03:30.690 --> 01:03:33.780 align:middle line:84%
of SafeInsights is not to
bring all the data together.

01:03:33.780 --> 01:03:36.880 align:middle line:84%
So it doesn't propose that
all of it gets collected.

01:03:36.880 --> 01:03:39.510 align:middle line:84%
One of the main things that
they want to be able to do

01:03:39.510 --> 01:03:43.470 align:middle line:84%
is to be able to conduct
research queries across data

01:03:43.470 --> 01:03:46.600 align:middle line:84%
without having people have to
directly access those data.

01:03:46.600 --> 01:03:49.260 align:middle line:84%
So there's a whole field
of study called things

01:03:49.260 --> 01:03:50.970 align:middle line:84%
like differential
privacy and stuff

01:03:50.970 --> 01:03:54.240 align:middle line:84%
like that, which
basically says, how

01:03:54.240 --> 01:03:56.680 align:middle line:84%
do I tell you the kinds of
things that are in a data set,

01:03:56.680 --> 01:04:00.180 align:middle line:84%
let you run a query
over that data set,

01:04:00.180 --> 01:04:02.770 align:middle line:84%
but never actually
encounter that data set?

01:04:02.770 --> 01:04:04.500 align:middle line:84%
And in this kind of
environment, there

01:04:04.500 --> 01:04:07.050 align:middle line:84%
might be, how would
you run a query that

01:04:07.050 --> 01:04:10.170 align:middle line:84%
connects people's student
behavior in a learning

01:04:10.170 --> 01:04:14.460 align:middle line:84%
platform with their registrar
information with their state

01:04:14.460 --> 01:04:18.240 align:middle line:84%
test scores without having to
make one giant data set out

01:04:18.240 --> 01:04:19.740 align:middle line:84%
of all those things
but, instead,

01:04:19.740 --> 01:04:26.060 align:middle line:84%
to have the query just run
over the data it needs--

01:04:26.060 --> 01:04:28.560 align:middle line:84%
have the people who own that
data still hold on to that data

01:04:28.560 --> 01:04:31.610 align:middle line:84%
but have the results of
that query go to technology

01:04:31.610 --> 01:04:33.803 align:middle line:84%
developers, researchers,
things like that,

01:04:33.803 --> 01:04:35.970 align:middle line:84%
without having to have them
collect all of the data?

01:04:35.970 --> 01:04:38.600 align:middle line:84%
So in some ways, it's
explicitly trying

01:04:38.600 --> 01:04:40.010 align:middle line:90%
to be the opposite of inBloom.

01:04:40.010 --> 01:04:41.840 align:middle line:84%
Don't bring all the
data in one place.

01:04:41.840 --> 01:04:44.570 align:middle line:84%
Try to get the benefits
of aggregated data

01:04:44.570 --> 01:04:47.392 align:middle line:84%
without having to share it with
either a trusted third party

01:04:47.392 --> 01:04:48.850 align:middle line:84%
or everyone or
something like that.

01:04:48.850 --> 01:04:51.438 align:middle line:90%


01:04:51.438 --> 01:04:51.980 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Oh.

01:04:51.980 --> 01:04:53.022 align:middle line:90%
I was just going to say--

01:04:53.022 --> 01:04:55.017 align:middle line:84%
I feel like you
answered it right there

01:04:55.017 --> 01:04:56.100 align:middle line:90%
with what you were saying.

01:04:56.100 --> 01:04:58.430 align:middle line:84%
But in your opinion,
do you think

01:04:58.430 --> 01:05:04.310 align:middle line:84%
the people who are creating
this, the SafeInsights,

01:05:04.310 --> 01:05:06.890 align:middle line:84%
do you think they really
understood what went wrong

01:05:06.890 --> 01:05:07.530 align:middle line:90%
in inBloom?

01:05:07.530 --> 01:05:10.040 align:middle line:84%
And do you think
like, ultimately,

01:05:10.040 --> 01:05:11.815 align:middle line:90%
does it lead back [INAUDIBLE]?

01:05:11.815 --> 01:05:15.740 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Boy, it
sure sounds like it to me.

01:05:15.740 --> 01:05:19.340 align:middle line:84%
Powerful, privacy-preserving
learning research.

01:05:19.340 --> 01:05:24.170 align:middle line:84%
So the very first concept they
want to ingrain in your head

01:05:24.170 --> 01:05:25.580 align:middle line:90%
is safe.

01:05:25.580 --> 01:05:29.720 align:middle line:84%
The second concept that I think
they're doing rhetorically

01:05:29.720 --> 01:05:33.230 align:middle line:84%
is leaning into insight
rather than data.

01:05:33.230 --> 01:05:35.990 align:middle line:84%
So I think if you asked
people what inBloom

01:05:35.990 --> 01:05:39.020 align:middle line:84%
was, they would have described
it as some kind of data

01:05:39.020 --> 01:05:40.050 align:middle line:90%
repository.

01:05:40.050 --> 01:05:44.450 align:middle line:84%
My hunch is that the
SafeInsights people

01:05:44.450 --> 01:05:48.710 align:middle line:84%
are going to describe themselves
as an insight generator,

01:05:48.710 --> 01:05:50.160 align:middle line:90%
as a research producer.

01:05:50.160 --> 01:05:52.220 align:middle line:84%
Now, it's the exact
same thing, right?

01:05:52.220 --> 01:05:53.970 align:middle line:84%
Because in order to
get the insights,

01:05:53.970 --> 01:05:55.650 align:middle line:84%
you have to have
the data somewhere.

01:05:55.650 --> 01:05:59.790 align:middle line:84%
And inBloom's purpose was
never just to hoard data.

01:05:59.790 --> 01:06:02.180 align:middle line:84%
It was to make learning
better for kids.

01:06:02.180 --> 01:06:05.090 align:middle line:90%
But I think there are--

01:06:05.090 --> 01:06:09.860 align:middle line:84%
I guarantee you that
many of the people who

01:06:09.860 --> 01:06:12.650 align:middle line:84%
worked on SafeInsights
have done plenty

01:06:12.650 --> 01:06:15.440 align:middle line:84%
of studying of what
went wrong with inBloom

01:06:15.440 --> 01:06:19.220 align:middle line:84%
and are designing this
enterprise so that--

01:06:19.220 --> 01:06:20.810 align:middle line:90%
it's with a trusted University.

01:06:20.810 --> 01:06:22.310 align:middle line:84%
It's founded by the
National Science

01:06:22.310 --> 01:06:25.390 align:middle line:84%
Foundation, which I assume
also has very high trust.

01:06:25.390 --> 01:06:28.560 align:middle line:84%
But all this language,
it definitely

01:06:28.560 --> 01:06:32.580 align:middle line:84%
strikes me as people who read
up on the inBloom case study

01:06:32.580 --> 01:06:35.730 align:middle line:90%
and are trying a different tack.

01:06:35.730 --> 01:06:38.887 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think
another good thing

01:06:38.887 --> 01:06:40.470 align:middle line:84%
that they did was
associate themselves

01:06:40.470 --> 01:06:42.910 align:middle line:84%
with a previous product
that was successful,

01:06:42.910 --> 01:06:46.420 align:middle line:84%
which was OpenStax, which,
in itself, OpenStax,

01:06:46.420 --> 01:06:48.390 align:middle line:90%
they are open--

01:06:48.390 --> 01:06:52.570 align:middle line:84%
what is it open-source,
free-to-use textbooks.

01:06:52.570 --> 01:06:54.810 align:middle line:84%
And that, in itself, has
proven to be like, OK, we

01:06:54.810 --> 01:06:57.060 align:middle line:84%
are transparent, quite
literally transparent,

01:06:57.060 --> 01:06:58.090 align:middle line:90%
about what we're doing.

01:06:58.090 --> 01:07:00.600 align:middle line:84%
And we've already provided
a useful, successful product

01:07:00.600 --> 01:07:02.440 align:middle line:90%
for the masses.

01:07:02.440 --> 01:07:07.350 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: And
they have a ton--

01:07:07.350 --> 01:07:11.820 align:middle line:84%
they have 40 partners, 39
collaborating institutions.

01:07:11.820 --> 01:07:13.450 align:middle line:84%
Of their collaborating
institutions,

01:07:13.450 --> 01:07:16.330 align:middle line:84%
77% are minority-serving
institutions.

01:07:16.330 --> 01:07:18.780 align:middle line:84%
So they're trying to start
from the beginning, saying,

01:07:18.780 --> 01:07:21.400 align:middle line:84%
we're not just going to do this
to improve Columbia and Rice.

01:07:21.400 --> 01:07:22.950 align:middle line:84%
We're going to do
this to improve

01:07:22.950 --> 01:07:26.400 align:middle line:84%
lots of different
kinds of organizations.

01:07:26.400 --> 01:07:32.250 align:middle line:84%
It also, instead of being
a consortium of states,

01:07:32.250 --> 01:07:37.750 align:middle line:84%
it's really mostly a consortium
of digital learning platforms.

01:07:37.750 --> 01:07:41.070 align:middle line:90%
It probably matters, too--

01:07:41.070 --> 01:07:46.027 align:middle line:84%
there's clear
emphasis on college.

01:07:46.027 --> 01:07:47.860 align:middle line:84%
ASSISTments doesn't do
anything for college.

01:07:47.860 --> 01:07:49.650 align:middle line:84%
So they definitely have
some things that are not--

01:07:49.650 --> 01:07:51.250 align:middle line:84%
I'm not sure it's
literally true,

01:07:51.250 --> 01:07:55.950 align:middle line:84%
but ASSISTments, 99% of what
they do is serve K-12 students.

01:07:55.950 --> 01:08:00.840 align:middle line:84%
But I think it maybe does
make a difference to--

01:08:00.840 --> 01:08:02.440 align:middle line:90%
college students are adults.

01:08:02.440 --> 01:08:04.280 align:middle line:84%
College students opt
in to go to college.

01:08:04.280 --> 01:08:06.280 align:middle line:84%
The state doesn't force
anyone to go to college.

01:08:06.280 --> 01:08:10.540 align:middle line:84%
And so you're just operating
in, maybe, illusory choice,

01:08:10.540 --> 01:08:12.480 align:middle line:84%
but you're operating
in a space where

01:08:12.480 --> 01:08:14.880 align:middle line:84%
you feel like people have
more agency, to begin with,

01:08:14.880 --> 01:08:16.060 align:middle line:90%
to operate.

01:08:16.060 --> 01:08:19.164 align:middle line:90%


01:08:19.164 --> 01:08:23.640 align:middle line:84%
Unlike inBloom, which said these
11 states are the organization--

01:08:23.640 --> 01:08:26.069 align:middle line:84%
these state education agencies
are the key people we're

01:08:26.069 --> 01:08:28.680 align:middle line:84%
interacting with,
they don't mention

01:08:28.680 --> 01:08:30.907 align:middle line:84%
higher education
accreditors or state boards

01:08:30.907 --> 01:08:31.740 align:middle line:90%
of higher education.

01:08:31.740 --> 01:08:33.198 align:middle line:84%
They basically have
left government

01:08:33.198 --> 01:08:35.670 align:middle line:84%
out, except from the
National Science Foundation,

01:08:35.670 --> 01:08:37.029 align:middle line:90%
of this development.

01:08:37.029 --> 01:08:38.609 align:middle line:84%
Maybe people are
more trustworthy

01:08:38.609 --> 01:08:40.180 align:middle line:90%
of these organizations.

01:08:40.180 --> 01:08:41.189 align:middle line:90%
Or maybe they're just--

01:08:41.189 --> 01:08:42.689 align:middle line:84%
even if they're not
more trustworthy

01:08:42.689 --> 01:08:47.100 align:middle line:84%
of these organizations,
critics are potentially

01:08:47.100 --> 01:08:48.779 align:middle line:84%
drawn to things
like New York State

01:08:48.779 --> 01:08:55.029 align:middle line:84%
approves X versus this kind of
arbitrary group of universities,

01:08:55.029 --> 01:08:58.649 align:middle line:84%
some of whom might be
in New York, approve X.

01:08:58.649 --> 01:09:01.560 align:middle line:84%
So it'll be super
interesting to watch.

01:09:01.560 --> 01:09:05.130 align:middle line:84%
The grant that they
got was from the same--

01:09:05.130 --> 01:09:07.830 align:middle line:84%
$90 million is probably
the largest federal grant

01:09:07.830 --> 01:09:10.612 align:middle line:84%
to an educational
research project

01:09:10.612 --> 01:09:13.279 align:middle line:84%
ever, although there's a couple
of educational research projects

01:09:13.279 --> 01:09:15.029 align:middle line:84%
that if you totaled
up all the grants they

01:09:15.029 --> 01:09:17.760 align:middle line:84%
got over a period
of time might be

01:09:17.760 --> 01:09:20.430 align:middle line:90%
at least within that ballpark.

01:09:20.430 --> 01:09:25.290 align:middle line:84%
It came from the same grant
funding that supports telescopes

01:09:25.290 --> 01:09:26.970 align:middle line:90%
and ecological plots.

01:09:26.970 --> 01:09:29.560 align:middle line:84%
It's called Mid-scale
Research Infrastructure,

01:09:29.560 --> 01:09:31.979 align:middle line:90%
things that are like--

01:09:31.979 --> 01:09:35.508 align:middle line:84%
no one university can build
a giant radio telescope.

01:09:35.508 --> 01:09:37.050 align:middle line:84%
And you don't want
one university to.

01:09:37.050 --> 01:09:39.580 align:middle line:84%
You want to build a thing that
the entire field of astronomy

01:09:39.580 --> 01:09:41.700 align:middle line:84%
can all collectively
use together.

01:09:41.700 --> 01:09:45.240 align:middle line:84%
And so this grant stream is
meant to build enormous things

01:09:45.240 --> 01:09:47.200 align:middle line:90%
that everyone can use together.

01:09:47.200 --> 01:09:50.840 align:middle line:84%
So it'll be interesting to see
what they do in the decades

01:09:50.840 --> 01:09:51.340 align:middle line:90%
ahead.

01:09:51.340 --> 01:09:57.630 align:middle line:84%
But I think, in some ways,
especially coming about 10 years

01:09:57.630 --> 01:10:02.370 align:middle line:84%
after the collapse of
inBloom, to me, it's neat.

01:10:02.370 --> 01:10:06.270 align:middle line:84%
People still have optimism
that if you pull together

01:10:06.270 --> 01:10:09.035 align:middle line:84%
a bunch of student learning
data and data about students,

01:10:09.035 --> 01:10:10.660 align:middle line:84%
and you conduct a
bunch of experiments,

01:10:10.660 --> 01:10:13.020 align:middle line:84%
you can build things that
help them learn better.

01:10:13.020 --> 01:10:17.920 align:middle line:84%
But there are
enormous real risks.

01:10:17.920 --> 01:10:21.780 align:middle line:84%
And there may be even more
enormous reputational risks

01:10:21.780 --> 01:10:23.380 align:middle line:90%
that go into this work.

01:10:23.380 --> 01:10:25.990 align:middle line:84%
One thing that I've
tried to communicate,

01:10:25.990 --> 01:10:29.310 align:middle line:84%
which it sounds like
these folks are adopting,

01:10:29.310 --> 01:10:31.590 align:middle line:90%
to many different groups--

01:10:31.590 --> 01:10:33.868 align:middle line:84%
because I'm basically part
of the community of people

01:10:33.868 --> 01:10:36.160 align:middle line:84%
that would like to collect
lots of data about students.

01:10:36.160 --> 01:10:39.720 align:middle line:84%
And I've handled
data files that have

01:10:39.720 --> 01:10:41.220 align:middle line:84%
way more information
about people

01:10:41.220 --> 01:10:43.740 align:middle line:84%
than I feel comfortable
having access to.

01:10:43.740 --> 01:10:46.180 align:middle line:84%
And part of the job is
doing things responsibly.

01:10:46.180 --> 01:10:48.510 align:middle line:84%
But the other part of
the job is constantly

01:10:48.510 --> 01:10:51.090 align:middle line:84%
talking to the public
about what we're doing.

01:10:51.090 --> 01:10:53.670 align:middle line:84%
The only way people
will not be creeped out

01:10:53.670 --> 01:10:57.390 align:middle line:84%
by doing experiments
is if, A, we

01:10:57.390 --> 01:10:59.760 align:middle line:84%
conduct a bunch of experiments
that meaningfully improve

01:10:59.760 --> 01:11:00.720 align:middle line:90%
learning.

01:11:00.720 --> 01:11:04.800 align:middle line:84%
A huge challenge that
education has is we

01:11:04.800 --> 01:11:07.360 align:middle line:84%
don't have the equivalent
of the Human Genome Project.

01:11:07.360 --> 01:11:08.860 align:middle line:84%
We don't have a
thing that you could

01:11:08.860 --> 01:11:10.710 align:middle line:84%
point to being
like, well, yeah, we

01:11:10.710 --> 01:11:13.540 align:middle line:84%
took a whole lot of your data,
but we're curing cancer here,

01:11:13.540 --> 01:11:15.370 align:middle line:90%
people.

01:11:15.370 --> 01:11:17.850 align:middle line:84%
If SafeInsights came
up with something

01:11:17.850 --> 01:11:21.070 align:middle line:84%
that made students
learn math 33% faster,

01:11:21.070 --> 01:11:23.010 align:middle line:84%
it would totally
change the way people

01:11:23.010 --> 01:11:24.665 align:middle line:90%
think about the collection of--

01:11:24.665 --> 01:11:26.040 align:middle line:84%
just your cost-benefit
data would

01:11:26.040 --> 01:11:28.950 align:middle line:90%
transform quite dramatically.

01:11:28.950 --> 01:11:31.980 align:middle line:84%
But even in the absence of
that, it's incredibly important

01:11:31.980 --> 01:11:34.500 align:middle line:84%
to be engaged with these
student privacy groups, engaged

01:11:34.500 --> 01:11:36.540 align:middle line:84%
with families, trying
to explain to people

01:11:36.540 --> 01:11:39.930 align:middle line:84%
there's always going to
be variation in education.

01:11:39.930 --> 01:11:43.530 align:middle line:84%
What we probably could benefit
the most from is systematic

01:11:43.530 --> 01:11:46.140 align:middle line:84%
variation, where we can tell
which parts of the variation

01:11:46.140 --> 01:11:47.530 align:middle line:90%
work and which parts don't.

01:11:47.530 --> 01:11:49.033 align:middle line:84%
And the only way
that we can do that

01:11:49.033 --> 01:11:50.700 align:middle line:84%
is through some of
these approaches that

01:11:50.700 --> 01:11:52.530 align:middle line:84%
include data collection and
randomization and things

01:11:52.530 --> 01:11:53.233 align:middle line:90%
like that.

01:11:53.233 --> 01:11:54.900 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So you just
made the statement

01:11:54.900 --> 01:11:59.290 align:middle line:84%
that SafeInsights comes
up with some solution.

01:11:59.290 --> 01:12:04.830 align:middle line:84%
So is SafeInsights, is it
like a third party that is--

01:12:04.830 --> 01:12:07.122 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: It is less of
a third party than inBloom.

01:12:07.122 --> 01:12:08.580 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is it
actual researchers?

01:12:08.580 --> 01:12:09.690 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yes.

01:12:09.690 --> 01:12:12.270 align:middle line:84%
Clearly, some of
the things that Rice

01:12:12.270 --> 01:12:15.370 align:middle line:84%
is going to do with this
money is build systems

01:12:15.370 --> 01:12:19.853 align:middle line:84%
and study OpenStax with it,
much more so than inBloom.

01:12:19.853 --> 01:12:23.110 align:middle line:90%


01:12:23.110 --> 01:12:25.000 align:middle line:84%
inBloom never, to
my knowledge, never

01:12:25.000 --> 01:12:29.650 align:middle line:84%
had a list of partners
that looked like this list.

01:12:29.650 --> 01:12:32.950 align:middle line:84%
They never said, from the
beginning, oh, look, Kahoot!

01:12:32.950 --> 01:12:36.280 align:middle line:84%
and Google Classroom and
all these other people

01:12:36.280 --> 01:12:39.130 align:middle line:84%
are our lead partners
and are going

01:12:39.130 --> 01:12:40.640 align:middle line:90%
to use this to figure out stuff.

01:12:40.640 --> 01:12:43.630 align:middle line:84%
They really saw
themselves solving

01:12:43.630 --> 01:12:48.271 align:middle line:84%
an infrastructural
problem more that--

01:12:48.271 --> 01:12:50.620 align:middle line:84%
I think these folks are
positioning themselves

01:12:50.620 --> 01:12:54.970 align:middle line:84%
more like, first, we're going
to have a set of tech products

01:12:54.970 --> 01:12:59.930 align:middle line:84%
that improve learning through
better research practices,

01:12:59.930 --> 01:13:03.070 align:middle line:84%
including better forms of data
management and data sharing

01:13:03.070 --> 01:13:04.900 align:middle line:90%
and data privacy.

01:13:04.900 --> 01:13:07.630 align:middle line:84%
But if it's a real
infrastructure product,

01:13:07.630 --> 01:13:08.470 align:middle line:90%
part of what--

01:13:08.470 --> 01:13:13.270 align:middle line:84%
I'm sure Rich's goal
is to say, we're

01:13:13.270 --> 01:13:16.120 align:middle line:84%
going to develop these
privacy-protecting research

01:13:16.120 --> 01:13:16.940 align:middle line:90%
enclaves.

01:13:16.940 --> 01:13:19.600 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to develop
them in an open-source way

01:13:19.600 --> 01:13:22.805 align:middle line:84%
that whatever future
EdTech companies come along

01:13:22.805 --> 01:13:25.180 align:middle line:84%
are going to be able to benefit
from this infrastructure.

01:13:25.180 --> 01:13:27.040 align:middle line:84%
They're going to be able to do
all the same kinds of things

01:13:27.040 --> 01:13:28.720 align:middle line:84%
that OpenStax is
doing to conduct

01:13:28.720 --> 01:13:31.360 align:middle line:84%
cool experimental
research with lots of data

01:13:31.360 --> 01:13:34.460 align:middle line:84%
while protecting
people's privacy.

01:13:34.460 --> 01:13:34.960 align:middle line:90%
Great.

01:13:34.960 --> 01:13:37.640 align:middle line:84%
It's 2:25, so I
should let you go.

01:13:37.640 --> 01:13:40.570 align:middle line:90%
We'll come in on Wednesday.

01:13:40.570 --> 01:13:43.420 align:middle line:84%
And you have a little bit of
reading about implementation

01:13:43.420 --> 01:13:44.450 align:middle line:90%
in schools.

01:13:44.450 --> 01:13:46.690 align:middle line:84%
But mostly, what you
should be working

01:13:46.690 --> 01:13:52.838 align:middle line:84%
on is getting some words on
paper for your final project

01:13:52.838 --> 01:13:54.380 align:middle line:84%
and thinking a little
bit about-- oh,

01:13:54.380 --> 01:13:56.757 align:middle line:84%
a thing that we'll do
probably by email this week,

01:13:56.757 --> 01:13:58.340 align:middle line:84%
but maybe a bit in
class on Wednesday,

01:13:58.340 --> 01:14:00.340 align:middle line:84%
is I need you to sign
up to either present

01:14:00.340 --> 01:14:01.752 align:middle line:90%
on Monday or Wednesday.

01:14:01.752 --> 01:14:03.460 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll send you
something pretty soon

01:14:03.460 --> 01:14:07.500 align:middle line:84%
that invites you to sign up
for one of those two slots.

01:14:07.500 --> 01:14:13.000 align:middle line:90%